						<section class="rt-box">
							<header class="rt-head"><span class="rt-step">4</span><div><strong>Is it practical?</strong><small>compiler conformance + measured cost against hand-written C#</small></div></header>
							<p class="rt-intro">The usual objection to a business-rules language is that it will be slow, because most of them are <strong>interpreters</strong> — at runtime they walk a tree of nodes, asking "what kind of node is this?" over and over for every decision. CLARA does not do that. It is a real compiler: a policy ends up as <strong>machine code</strong>, reached by the same route your C# takes.</p>

							<div class="pipeline-chain">
								<span>CLARA source</span><span>tokens</span><span>AST</span><span>bound + typed</span><span>expression tree</span><span>CIL</span><span>machine code</span>
							</div>

							<table class="audit-table">
								<thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Stage</th><th>What happens</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead>
								<tbody>
									<tr><td>1</td><td>Lex</td><td>The policy text is split into tokens — keywords, names, numbers, operators — each carrying its line and column.</td><td>Positions survive the whole pipeline, which is why an error can point at a line rather than at "the policy".</td></tr>
									<tr><td>2</td><td>Parse</td><td>Tokens become an <em>abstract syntax tree</em>: the nested shape of rules, conditions and arithmetic.</td><td>Structure is now known, but nothing has been checked for meaning yet.</td></tr>
									<tr><td>3</td><td>Bind &amp; type-check</td><td>Names are resolved, types are verified, unreachable rules are found, constant arithmetic is folded, and every input, derive and output is assigned a fixed <strong>slot in a <code>decimal[]</code> array</strong>.</td><td>This is the stage most rule engines skip. It produces the <code>CLARA001–009</code> diagnostics, and the slot allocation is what removes dictionary lookups at runtime.</td></tr>
									<tr><td>4</td><td>Lower</td><td>The checked tree is rewritten into a <strong>.NET expression tree</strong>: rules become a chain of <code>Expression.Condition</code>, assignments become writes into the array slots.</td><td>Now it is an ordinary .NET program description, not a CLARA-specific structure.</td></tr>
									<tr><td>5</td><td>Emit</td><td><code>Expression.Lambda&lt;Func&lt;decimal[], int&gt;&gt;(…).Compile()</code> emits <strong>CIL</strong> — the .NET intermediate language — into a dynamic method.</td><td>This is the "codegen" step. The output is the same bytecode a C# compiler would produce.</td></tr>
									<tr><td>6</td><td>JIT</td><td>On first call, the runtime's just-in-time compiler turns that CIL into native <strong>machine code</strong> for this CPU.</td><td>Identical treatment to hand-written C#. Nothing about the code's origin is visible to the JIT.</td></tr>
									<tr><td>7</td><td>Run</td><td>What is left is a <code>Func&lt;decimal[], int&gt;</code> delegate: pass the inputs in the array, get back the index of the rule that fired, read the outputs from their slots.</td><td>A policy call is a normal function call, not a tree walk.</td></tr>
								</tbody>
							</table>

							<p class="rt-reco">The step people usually leave out when sketching this is <strong>3</strong>. It is tempting to read the chain as "AST → intermediate language → machine code", but bind-and-type-check sits in the middle, and it is where every safety guarantee is actually enforced — a policy that fails there never reaches codegen at all. The other common slip is the word "intermediate language": that means <strong>CIL</strong>, which is emitted at step 5, and it is still not machine code until step 6.</p>

							<p class="ctx-method">Two honest limits. First, this holds only on a runtime with a JIT; under NativeAOT or on platforms that forbid runtime code generation, <code>.Compile()</code> falls back to an interpreter and these numbers would not survive. Second, arithmetic is <code>decimal</code> throughout, because money must not be held in binary floating point — that choice sets the performance floor, and hand-written C# pays exactly the same cost, which is what makes the comparison below fair.</p>

							<p class="rt-intro">The button runs the compiler's own conformance suite, then times the reference late-fee policy against the identical rules written by hand in C# — on this machine, right now.</p>
							<div class="rt-actions">
								<small>@(_benchmark is null ? "not measured yet" : _benchmark.Runtime)</small>
								<button class="primary-command compact" type="button" data-auto="s16.benchmark" disabled="@_benchmarkRunning" @onclick="RunBenchmark">@(_benchmarkRunning ? "Measuring…" : "Run conformance + benchmark")</button>
							</div>

							@if (_conformance is { } conformance)
							{
								<div class="@(conformance.Passed == conformance.Total ? "compare-status" : "compare-status failed")" role="status">
									Compiler conformance: @conformance.Passed / @conformance.Total guarantees held (@conformance.DurationMilliseconds.ToString("N0") ms).
								</div>
								@if (conformance.Passed != conformance.Total)
								{
									<ul class="rt-list warn">@foreach (var item in conformance.Cases.Where(c => !c.Passed)) { <li>@item.Name — @item.Detail</li> }</ul>
								}
							}

							@if (_benchmark is { } measurement)
							{
								<table class="audit-table" data-auto="s16.benchmark-result">
									<thead><tr><th>Engine</th><th>ns / decision</th><th>Million decisions / sec</th><th>vs C#</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
									<tbody>
										@foreach (var row in measurement.Rows)
										{
											<tr>
												<td>@row.Engine</td>
												<td>@row.NanosecondsPerCall.ToString("N1")</td>
												<td>@row.MillionCallsPerSecond.ToString("N1")</td>
												<td>@row.RelativeToCSharp.ToString("N2")x</td>
												<td>@row.Detail</td>
											</tr>
										}
									</tbody>
								</table>
								<p class="rt-reco">@measurement.Summary</p>
								<small>Policy @measurement.PolicyName compiled once in @measurement.CompileMilliseconds.ToString("N2") ms, then executed @measurement.Iterations.ToString("N0") times per round, best of @measurement.Rounds rounds after warm-up. Output parity verified on @measurement.ParityChecks.ToString("N0") inputs with @measurement.ParityMismatches mismatches.</small>
							}
							else if (!_benchmarkRunning)
							{
								<div class="rt-placeholder">Run the benchmark to see whether the language is fast enough to sit on a hot path.</div>
							}
						</section>
					</section>
					break;
