				case 16:
					<section>
						<p class="kicker">GROUNDING, GRADED BY A COMPILER</p>
						<h2 id="scene-title">Three migrations of real Enterprise COBOL</h2>
						<p class="rt-intro">This is the monthly-interest calculation from <strong>AWS CardDemo</strong> — genuine IBM Enterprise COBOL, not a toy. Field layouts come from copybooks <code>CVTRA01Y</code> and <code>CVTRA02Y</code>; the arithmetic is <code>CBACT04C</code> paragraph <code>1300-COMPUTE-INTEREST</code>, unchanged. The same model migrates it three times with increasing grounding: <strong>none</strong>, <strong>static-analysis artifacts</strong>, then <strong>facts resolved by the COBOL compiler</strong>. Every answer is executed against output captured from the real program. <strong>No model grades anything.</strong></p>

						<div class="rt-box">
							<header class="rt-head"><span class="rt-step">!</span><div><strong>Why a COBOL compiler, and why the IBM dialect</strong><small>the point of the whole tab</small></div></header>
							<div style="padding:14px">
								<p class="rt-intro">A model has read enormous amounts of C and very little COBOL — and almost none of it <em>Enterprise</em> COBOL, because that code lives inside banks and insurers and never reaches the public internet. But the real problem is not fluency. <strong>Some COBOL meaning is not in the source text at all.</strong></p>
								<p class="rt-intro">Look at the one line this tab migrates:</p>
								<pre class="rt-code">COMPUTE WS-MONTHLY-INT = (TRAN-CAT-BAL * DIS-INT-RATE) / 1200</pre>
								<p class="rt-intro">There is no <code>ROUNDED</code>, so the result <strong>truncates</strong>. On a balance of $1,000.00 at 18.99% the exact answer is <strong>15.825</strong> and the program pays <strong>15.82</strong>. A migration that rounds pays <strong>15.83</strong> — one cent, on every account, every month. Nothing in the source says “truncate”. The COBOL standard says it, and the compiler implements it.</p>
								<p class="rt-intro"><strong>The dialect is not cosmetic either.</strong> The same program, the same input, compiled under different dialects:</p>
								<pre class="rt-code">MOVE 50000 TO WS-BIN.   *&gt; PIC 9(4) COMP

default  -&gt; 000000      truncates to 4 decimal digits
ibm      -&gt; 050000      keeps the binary value (TRUNC(BIN))
mf       -&gt; 050000
cobol85  -&gt; 000000</pre>
								<p class="rt-intro">Zero versus fifty thousand, with no error and no warning. So the compiler is only an oracle <strong>once you tell it which COBOL you mean</strong>. Run it under the wrong dialect and it becomes a confident, silent liar. This tab compiles with <code>-std=ibm</code> because the source is IBM Enterprise COBOL.</p>
							</div>
						</div>

						@if (_session?.Migration is { } migration)
						{
							<div class="@(migration.ToolchainAvailable ? "compare-status" : "compare-status failed")" role="status">@migration.ToolchainStatus</div>

							<section class="rt-box">
								<header class="rt-head"><span class="rt-step">1</span><div><strong>The legacy program</strong><small>AWS CardDemo CBACT04C · Apache-2.0 · REDEFINES overlay, implied decimals, unrounded COMPUTE</small></div></header>
								<pre class="rt-code">@migration.CobolSource</pre>
							</section>

							<section class="rt-box">
								<header class="rt-head"><span class="rt-step">2</span><div><strong>The oracle</strong><small>the compiled COBOL, executed — this is the ground truth</small></div></header>
								@if (migration.Oracle.Count > 0)
								{
									<table class="audit-table">
										<thead><tr><th>Input record</th><th>Legacy output</th></tr></thead>
										<tbody>
											@foreach (var run in migration.Oracle)
											{
												<tr><td><code>@run.Input</code></td><td>@(string.IsNullOrEmpty(run.Error) ? run.Output : run.Error)</td></tr>
											}
										</tbody>
									</table>
								}
								else
								{
									<div class="rt-placeholder">The oracle runs when you start the migration.</div>
								}

								@if (migration.CompilerFields.Count > 0)
								{
									<h4>What the compiler resolved (given only to arm C)</h4>
									<table class="audit-table">
										<thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Offset</th><th>Bytes</th><th>Type, digits, scale</th></tr></thead>
										<tbody>
											@foreach (var field in migration.CompilerFields)
											{
												<tr><td>@field.Name</td><td>@field.Offset</td><td>@field.Size</td><td>@field.Attribute</td></tr>
											}
										</tbody>
									</table>
								}
							</section>

							<section class="rt-box rt-input-box">
								<header class="rt-head"><span class="rt-step">3</span><div><strong>Run the comparison</strong><small>@migration.Arms.Count of 3 arms complete</small></div></header>
								<div class="rt-actions">
									<small>Gemma 4 migrates the same program three times</small>
									<button class="primary-command compact" type="button" data-auto="s18.run" disabled="@(!_bridgeConnected || MigrationBusy || !migration.ToolchainAvailable)" @onclick="RunMigration">@MigrationButtonText</button>
								</div>
								@if (!_bridgeConnected)
								{
									<p class="bridge-required" role="status">Connect the VSIX with the token shown under Local Bridge before running.</p>
								}
							</section>

							@foreach (var arm in migration.Arms)
							{
								<section class="rt-box">
									<header class="rt-head"><span class="rt-step">@arm.Arm</span><div><strong>Arm @arm.Arm — @GroundingLabel(arm.Grounding)</strong><small>@(arm.ModelUsed ?? arm.RequestedModel) · @arm.PromptTokens.ToString("N0") prompt tokens</small></div></header>
									@if (arm.Cases.Count > 0)
									{
										<div class="@(arm.Passed == arm.Cases.Count ? "compare-status" : "compare-status failed")" role="status">
											@arm.Passed of @arm.Cases.Count cases match the legacy program.
										</div>
										<table class="audit-table">
											<thead><tr><th>Input</th><th>Legacy says</th><th>This arm says</th><th></th></tr></thead>
											<tbody>
												@foreach (var item in arm.Cases)
												{
													<tr>
														<td><code>@item.Input</code></td>
														<td>@item.Expected</td>
														<td>@item.Actual@(string.IsNullOrEmpty(item.Note) ? "" : $" ({item.Note})")</td>
														<td>@(item.Matched ? "match" : "MISMATCH")</td>
													</tr>
												}
											</tbody>
										</table>
									}
									else if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(arm.Error))
									{
										<div class="compare-status failed" role="alert">@arm.Error</div>
										@if (arm.CompileErrors.Count > 0)
										{
											<ul class="rt-list warn">@foreach (var error in arm.CompileErrors) { <li>@error</li> }</ul>
										}
									}
									else if (arm.Status is "pending" or "running")
									{
										<div class="rt-pending"><span></span>Migrating…</div>
									}

									@if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(arm.Code))
									{
										<pre class="rt-code">@arm.Code</pre>
									}
								</section>
							}

							@if (migration.Status == "completed" && migration.Arms.Count == 3)
							{
								<section class="rt-qa">
									<header class="rt-head"><span class="rt-step">&#10003;</span><div><strong>Verdict</strong><small>decided by execution, not by a model</small></div></header>
									<table class="audit-table">
										<thead><tr><th>Arm</th><th>Grounding</th><th>Cases passed</th><th>Prompt tokens</th></tr></thead>
										<tbody>
											@foreach (var arm in migration.Arms)
											{
												<tr><td>@arm.Arm</td><td>@GroundingLabel(arm.Grounding)</td><td>@arm.Passed / @arm.Cases.Count</td><td>@arm.PromptTokens.ToString("N0")</td></tr>
											}
										</tbody>
									</table>
									<h4>Read this carefully</h4>
									<ul class="rt-list warn">
										<li>One program, one run per arm. This demonstrates a method; it does not measure a migration programme.</li>
										<li>The case set covers the branches we thought of. A boundary we did not think of is not covered — note that a deliberately wrong implementation still passes most of these cases.</li>
										<li>The target is C# rather than Java because the server can execute C# deterministically. The grounding question is the same either way.</li>
										<li>Arm C's advantage, if any, comes from facts the compiler resolved — not from the compiler's C being easier to read. It is not; it is <code>goto</code> chains and <code>cob_decimal</code> calls.</li>
										<li><strong>The oracle is only as right as its dialect.</strong> Before trusting this against production, confirm the <code>TRUNC</code> option the mainframe build actually uses — <code>TRUNC(BIN)</code>, <code>TRUNC(STD)</code> or <code>TRUNC(OPT)</code>. Mismatch it and every number downstream inherits the error.</li>
									</ul>
								</section>
							}
						}
					</section>
					break;
				case 17:
					<section>
						<p class="kicker">MAP THE ESTATE BEFORE MIGRATING IT</p>
						<h2 id="scene-title">Identify COBOL domains</h2>
						<p class="rt-intro">
							Before a large COBOL estate can be modernised, someone has to answer a simple question that nobody can
							answer today: <strong>what business areas is this code actually made of, and where are the seams between
							them?</strong> This scene shows a tool that reads the COBOL programs, the shared record layouts
							(copybooks), the batch job control (JCL), the online transaction definitions (CICS) and the programmers'
							own notes, and produces a <strong>proposed domain map with the evidence attached</strong> &mdash; every
							claim points to a file and a line number.
						</p>
						<p class="rt-intro">
							<strong>No AI model decides anything on this page.</strong> Every step is ordinary, repeatable code that
							produces the same answer every time it runs. The only place a model could be used is to write a
							plain-English label for a group it did not create &mdash; and on this sample even that was not needed.
							Where the tool is not sure, it says so and hands the question to a person.
						</p>

						@{ var domains = CobolDomains.Run(); }

						@if (!domains.Available)
						{
							<div class="compare-status failed" role="alert">@domains.StatusMessage</div>
						}
						else
						{
							<div class="rt-box">
								<header class="rt-head"><span class="rt-step">?</span><div><strong>How to read this page</strong><small>each step answers three questions</small></div></header>
								<div class="audit-body">
									<p>
										Every numbered step below says <strong>what we did</strong>, <strong>why it matters</strong>, and
										<strong>what it found in this sample</strong>. The tables underneath each step are the evidence. You do not
										need to read them to follow the story; they are there so that anyone who wants to check a claim can.
									</p>
									<p>
										The sample is IBM's <strong>Global Auto Mart</strong> demonstration &mdash; a small car-dealership
										application, written in real IBM Enterprise COBOL for the mainframe, published by IBM on GitHub. A user
										picks a make of car, then a model, then sees the cars in stock and can pick one to see its details. It is
										five programs, five copybooks and two JCL decks: small enough that every line of the result can be checked
										by hand, which is exactly why it was chosen. <strong>Five programs cannot show us a real domain
										boundary</strong>; what they show is that every part of the machine works and reports honestly.
									</p>
									<table class="audit-table">
										<thead><tr><th>Member</th><th>What it is</th><th>What it does, in plain terms</th></tr></thead>
										<tbody>
											<tr><td><code>GAM0VMI</code></td><td>Online screen program</td><td>The &ldquo;choose a make, then a model&rdquo; screens. Asks <code>GAM0VMM</code> for the lists, then hands the user over to <code>GAM0VII</code>.</td></tr>
											<tr><td><code>GAM0VMM</code></td><td>Database lookup</td><td>Reads the <code>MAKE_MODEL</code> table: which makes exist and which models each make has &mdash; the <em>vehicle catalogue</em>.</td></tr>
											<tr><td><code>GAM0VII</code></td><td>Online screen program</td><td>The &ldquo;cars in stock&rdquo; screen. Asks <code>GAM0VSI</code> for the list; when a car is picked, hands over to <code>GAM0VDI</code> &mdash; a program IBM did not include in the sample.</td></tr>
											<tr><td><code>GAM0VSI</code></td><td>Database lookup</td><td>Reads the <code>EASTINVNTRY</code> table: the vehicles actually on the lot, with VIN, year, price and new/used &mdash; the <em>vehicle inventory</em>.</td></tr>
											<tr><td><code>GAM0VDB</code></td><td>Batch loader</td><td>Fills the database tables from data held in copybooks. Builds its SQL as text at run time, which matters later.</td></tr>
											<tr><td><code>GAM0BCA</code></td><td>Copybook</td><td>The <em>commarea</em>: the handful of fields (make chosen, model chosen, VIN chosen) carried from screen to screen. Shared by all four online programs.</td></tr>
											<tr><td><code>GAM0BMT</code>, <code>GAM0BET</code>, <code>GAM0BDT</code></td><td>Copybooks</td><td>Field-by-field layouts of the <code>MAKE_MODEL</code>, <code>EASTINVNTRY</code> and <code>DEALERSHIP</code> tables, generated by DB2's DCLGEN tool.</td></tr>
											<tr><td><code>GAM0BMD</code></td><td>Copybook</td><td>The make/model reference data the loader inserts.</td></tr>
											<tr><td><code>GAM0VCDB</code>, <code>GAMCSDUP</code></td><td>JCL</td><td>The batch job that creates the tables and runs the loader; the deck that defines the online transactions and tells CICS which program each one starts.</td></tr>
										</tbody>
									</table>
									<p class="rt-note">
										This table was written by a person after reading the source, so you know what you are looking at. Everything
										from Step 1 onward was produced by the tool, and you can check the two against each other.
									</p>
								</div>
							</div>

							<section class="rt-box" data-auto="dom.coverage">
								<header class="rt-head"><span class="rt-step">1</span><div><strong>How much of the code did we actually see?</strong><small>answered first, because every conclusion below depends on it</small></div></header>
								<div class="audit-body">
									<p>
										<strong>What we did.</strong> Before drawing any map, the tool counts what it could and could not read:
										programs, shared record layouts, database statements, and calls to other programs.
									</p>
									<p>
										<strong>Why it matters.</strong> A map drawn from 70% of the code is a claim about 70% of the code. If a
										program cannot be read it simply vanishes from the map, and a missing program makes the boundaries look
										cleaner than they really are. Putting these numbers first, in the same place every time, is how we stop
										a good-looking result from being an incomplete one.
									</p>
									<p>
										<strong>What it found here.</strong> Every program was read in full. The lower numbers are not failures
										of the tool: they are things IBM left out of the sample (system copybooks, screen layouts, one program) and
										one program that builds its database statements at run time. Each gap is named and explained in Step 2
										and Step 3, and each carries through to the map.
									</p>
									<table class="audit-table">
										<thead><tr><th>What we measured</th><th>Found</th><th>%</th><th>Why a gap here matters</th></tr></thead>
										<tbody>
											@foreach (var metric in domains.Coverage)
											{
												<tr class="@(metric.Percent >= 90 ? null : "p0-req")">
													<td>@metric.Name</td>
													<td>@metric.Resolved / @metric.Total</td>
													<td><strong>@metric.Percent%</strong></td>
													<td>@metric.Consequence</td>
												</tr>
											}
										</tbody>
									</table>
									<p class="rt-note">
										Sample: @domains.Members.Count member(s) from the IBM Global Auto Mart sample &mdash;
										@domains.Members.Count(m => m.Kind == "program") program(s),
										@domains.Members.Count(m => m.Kind == "copybook") copybook(s),
										@domains.Members.Count(m => m.Kind == "jcl") JCL. Pipeline ran in @domains.ElapsedMs ms.
									</p>
								</div>
							</section>

							<section class="rt-box">
								<header class="rt-head"><span class="rt-step">2</span><div><strong>Read the source the way the mainframe does</strong><small>pipeline stages S1&ndash;S3 &middot; @CobolFrontEnd.FrontEndName</small></div></header>
								<div class="audit-body">
									<p>
										<strong>What we did.</strong> Mainframe COBOL is written in a fixed 80-column card layout: columns 1&ndash;6
										are a line number, column 7 says whether the line is a comment or a continuation, columns 8&ndash;72 are the
										code, and columns 73&ndash;80 are a scratch area where programmers often park change-ticket numbers. The
										tool strips the layout, joins continued lines, keeps the column 73&ndash;80 text as metadata, and then
										pulls in every shared record layout (copybook) each program asks for &mdash; in the same search order the
										mainframe compiler would use.
									</p>
									<p>
										<strong>Why it matters.</strong> The parser we use expects modern free-format text, so skipping this step
										makes a perfectly good program look like hundreds of syntax errors. Copybook order matters too: if two
										libraries hold a copybook with the same name, the mainframe takes the first one it finds and says nothing.
										Get that wrong and every field offset in the analysis is wrong, silently. Most importantly, <strong>every
										line the tool produces remembers exactly which original line it came from</strong> &mdash; that is what
										lets every later claim cite a file and line number.
									</p>
									<p>
										<strong>What it found here.</strong> All @domains.Normalisation.Count members round-tripped cleanly. Of the
										@domains.Copies.Count copybook requests, @domains.Copies.Count(c => c.Resolved) were found in the sample. The
										other @domains.Copies.Count(c => !c.Resolved) requests (@domains.Copies.Where(c => !c.Resolved).Select(c => c.Requested).Distinct(StringComparer.OrdinalIgnoreCase).Count()
										distinct copybooks) are listed individually with the reason: large data-population files we deliberately left out
										of the fixture, IBM system copybooks that live in the CICS and DB2 libraries on the mainframe rather than in
										application source, and generated screen layouts. &ldquo;We chose to leave it out&rdquo; and &ldquo;we could
										not find it&rdquo; are different things, and the tool never blurs them.
									</p>
									<table class="audit-table">
										<thead><tr><th>Member</th><th>Lines</th><th>Code</th><th>Comment</th><th>Continuations joined</th><th>Cols 73&ndash;80 used</th><th>Traceable to source</th></tr></thead>
										<tbody>
											@foreach (var line in domains.Normalisation.OrderBy(r => r.Member, StringComparer.Ordinal))
											{
												<tr>
													<td><code>@line.Member</code></td>
													<td>@line.RawLines</td><td>@line.CodeLines</td><td>@line.CommentLines</td>
													<td>@line.ContinuationsJoined</td><td>@line.IdAreaPopulated</td>
													<td>@(line.RoundTripOk ? "yes" : line.Detail)</td>
												</tr>
											}
										</tbody>
									</table>

									<h4>Shared record layouts (copybooks) each program asked for</h4>
									<table class="audit-table">
										<thead><tr><th>Program</th><th>Copybook</th><th>Line</th><th>Found?</th></tr></thead>
										<tbody>
											@foreach (var copy in domains.Copies.OrderBy(c => c.Program, StringComparer.Ordinal).ThenBy(c => c.OriginalLine))
											{
												<tr class="@(copy.Resolved ? null : "p0-req")">
													<td><code>@copy.Program</code></td><td><code>@copy.Requested</code></td><td>@copy.OriginalLine</td>
													<td>@(copy.Resolved ? "yes" : copy.Reason)</td>
												</tr>
											}
										</tbody>
									</table>

									@if (domains.CopyOracle is { } oracle)
									{
										<div class="@(oracle.Passed ? "compare-status" : "compare-status failed")" role="status">
											<strong>Second opinion from a real COBOL compiler:</strong> @oracle.Detail
										</div>
										<p class="rt-note">
											We do not take our own word for the copybook results. An independent COBOL compiler (GnuCOBOL) is asked
											to find the same copybooks in the same folders, and its answer is compared with ours. Two different
											mechanisms agreeing is evidence; one mechanism asserting is not. The compiler cannot raise the percentage
											above, because it cannot find a file that is not on disk either.
										</p>
									}
								</div>
							</section>

							<section class="rt-box">
								<header class="rt-head"><span class="rt-step">3</span><div><strong>Find every database table and screen each program touches</strong><small>pipeline stage S2b &middot; @domains.ExecBlocks.Count embedded SQL / CICS command(s) read</small></div></header>
								<div class="audit-body">
									<p>
										<strong>What we did.</strong> Mainframe COBOL talks to the database (DB2) and to the online screen system
										(CICS) through embedded commands that the COBOL grammar itself treats as opaque text. The tool pulls
										those commands out and reads them with two small dedicated readers: one for SQL, one for CICS. From SQL it
										records which table each program <em>reads</em>, <em>writes</em>, <em>updates</em> or <em>deletes</em>.
										From CICS it records which programs call which, which screens they display, and which files they touch.
									</p>
									<p>
										<strong>Why it matters.</strong> <strong>Who writes which data is the single strongest clue to where a
										business domain begins and ends.</strong> A domain is the set of programs that owns and changes a set of
										data. A plan that assumes the COBOL parser will give you this table would produce an empty one, and
										nobody would notice until the domain boundaries looked absurd.
									</p>
									<p>
										<strong>What it found here.</strong> Two programs read two tables: <code>GAM0VMM</code> reads the vehicle
										catalogue (<code>MAKE_MODEL</code>) and <code>GAM0VSI</code> reads the vehicle inventory
										(<code>EASTINVNTRY</code>). The loader <code>GAM0VDB</code> shows up as <em>unknown</em>: it assembles its
										INSERT statements as text while running, so no amount of reading the source can say which tables it
										writes. The tool reports that honestly rather than guessing &mdash; and it means <strong>write ownership is
										unproven for every table in this sample</strong>, which is exactly the kind of gap a real estate will have
										and must be told about.
									</p>
									<table class="audit-table">
										<thead><tr><th>Program</th><th>Table or file</th><th>Does what</th><th>How sure</th><th>Evidence (file:line)</th></tr></thead>
										<tbody>
											@foreach (var entry in domains.Crud.OrderBy(c => c.Program, StringComparer.Ordinal).ThenBy(c => c.Artifact, StringComparer.Ordinal))
											{
												<tr class="@(entry.Confidence == "unresolved" ? "p0-req" : null)">
													<td><code>@entry.Program</code></td><td><code>@entry.Artifact</code></td>
													<td>@entry.Access</td><td>@entry.Confidence</td>
													<td><small>@entry.EvidenceMember:@entry.EvidenceLine</small></td>
												</tr>
											}
										</tbody>
									</table>
								</div>
							</section>

							<section class="rt-box">
								<header class="rt-head"><span class="rt-step">4</span><div><strong>Draw the map: who calls whom, who shares what</strong><small>pipeline stage S5 &middot; @domains.Nodes.Count things &middot; @domains.Edges.Count connections</small></div></header>
								<div class="audit-body">
									<p>
										<strong>What we did.</strong> Everything found so far is joined into one map. The <em>things</em> on the
										map are programs, copybooks, tables, screens and transactions. The <em>connections</em> are facts such as
										&ldquo;program A calls program B&rdquo;, &ldquo;program A includes copybook C&rdquo;, &ldquo;program A reads
										table T&rdquo;. Each connection carries a weight: a call that passes a large block of shared data couples
										two programs far more tightly than a call that passes a single return code, and the map reflects that.
									</p>
									<p>
										<strong>Why it matters.</strong> This map is what the grouping in Step 8 works from. Two things keep it
										honest. First, <strong>every connection names the file and line that proves it</strong>; a connection
										with no evidence is thrown out as a defect, not kept as a weak guess. Second, two kinds of CICS call that
										look similar are kept apart: <code>LINK</code> means &ldquo;call and come back&rdquo;, <code>XCTL</code>
										means &ldquo;hand over and do not come back&rdquo;. They shape the application differently.
									</p>
									<p>
										<strong>What it found here.</strong> The two screen programs each call their own database helper and never
										the other's &mdash; <code>GAM0VMI</code> calls <code>GAM0VMM</code>, <code>GAM0VII</code> calls
										<code>GAM0VSI</code>. The only link between the two halves is the hand-over from the make/model screens to
										the inventory screen. That is already the shape of two domains. One road leaves the map:
										<code>GAM0VII</code> hands over to <code>GAM0VDI</code>, which IBM did not include in the sample.
									</p>
									<table class="audit-table">
										<thead><tr><th>Kind of connection</th><th>Count</th><th>Proven exactly</th><th>Could not be resolved</th></tr></thead>
										<tbody>
											@foreach (var group in domains.Edges.GroupBy(e => e.Kind).OrderByDescending(g => g.Count()))
											{
												<tr>
													<td><code>@group.Key</code> &mdash; @EdgeKindGloss(group.Key)</td><td>@group.Count()</td>
													<td>@group.Count(e => e.Confidence == "exact")</td>
													<td>@group.Count(e => e.Confidence == "unresolved")</td>
												</tr>
											}
										</tbody>
									</table>
									<h4>Program-to-program calls</h4>
									<table class="audit-table">
										<thead><tr><th>From</th><th>How</th><th>To</th><th>Coupling weight</th><th>Evidence (file:line)</th></tr></thead>
										<tbody>
											@foreach (var edge in domains.Edges.Where(e => e.Kind is "CICS_LINK" or "CICS_XCTL" or "CALLS_STATIC" or "CALLS_DYNAMIC").OrderBy(e => e.Source, StringComparer.Ordinal).ThenBy(e => e.EvidenceLine))
											{
												<tr>
													<td><code>@edge.Source.Replace("program:", "")</code></td>
													<td>@EdgeKindGloss(edge.Kind)</td>
													<td><code>@edge.Target.Replace("program:", "")</code></td>
													<td>@edge.Weight</td>
													<td><small>@edge.EvidenceMember:@edge.EvidenceLine</small></td>
												</tr>
											}
										</tbody>
									</table>
									<p class="rt-note">
										Weight is <em>kind of call &times; log(1 + bytes of shared data passed)</em>. All four online programs pass the
										same commarea, so the weights here are uniform; on a real estate they are not, and an unweighted map lies.
									</p>
								</div>
							</section>

							<section class="rt-box" data-auto="dom.jcl">
								<header class="rt-head"><span class="rt-step">5</span><div><strong>Read the JCL and the CICS definitions too</strong><small>@domains.Jcl.Count fact(s) from @domains.Members.Count(m => m.Kind == "jcl") member(s) &middot; not in the COBOL at all</small></div></header>
								<div class="audit-body">
									<p>
										<strong>What we did.</strong> Some of the most important facts about a mainframe application are not
										written in COBOL. The batch job control (JCL) says which job step runs which program and in what order,
										and which database tables the job creates. The CICS resource deck says which screen transaction a user
										types to start which program. The tool reads both and lists what they say next to the COBOL.
									</p>
									<p>
										<strong>Why it matters.</strong> The COBOL cannot tell you that <code>GAM0VDB</code> is a batch program run
										once to load data, or that a user reaches <code>GAM0VMI</code> by typing <code>GBMI</code>. Without the
										JCL you would have the pieces but not the order they run in or the doors a user comes through. On a real
										estate, comparing the CICS deck with the source library is also the fastest way to find programs that are
										defined but missing, or present but never started.
									</p>
									<p>
										<strong>What it found here.</strong> Four transactions start the four online programs, confirming which
										programs are the entry points. The batch job creates five tables, but only two are read by anything in
										the sample; the loader is run as a batch step after the tables are created. The CICS deck also defines six
										programs described as PL/I that we were not given, and it does <em>not</em> define <code>GAM0VDI</code>
										&mdash; the program the inventory screen hands over to. These facts are folded into the findings at the end.
									</p>
									<table class="audit-table">
										<thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>Fact</th><th>Subject</th><th>Program / table</th><th>What that tells us</th><th>Line</th></tr></thead>
										<tbody>
											@foreach (var fact in domains.Jcl.Where(f => f.Kind is not "STEP").OrderBy(f => f.Member, StringComparer.Ordinal).ThenBy(f => f.OriginalLine))
											{
												<tr class="@(fact.Detail.Contains("not in the sample") || fact.Detail.Contains("no program") ? "p0-req" : null)">
													<td><code>@fact.Member</code></td><td>@JclKindGloss(fact.Kind)</td>
													<td><code>@fact.Subject</code></td><td><code>@fact.Target</code></td>
													<td>@fact.Detail</td><td>@fact.OriginalLine</td>
												</tr>
											}
										</tbody>
									</table>
									<p class="rt-note">
										At this size the JCL confirms what the COBOL already showed, so these facts are listed beside the map rather
										than weighted into the grouping. On a real estate, job-step order and transaction routing become their own
										grouping signals.
									</p>
								</div>
							</section>

							<section class="rt-box" data-auto="dom.pdg-box">
								<header class="rt-head"><span class="rt-step">6</span><div><strong>Look inside one program, on request</strong><small>pipeline stages S6&ndash;S7 &middot; built when you click, never stored</small></div></header>
								<div class="audit-body">
									<p>
										<strong>What we did.</strong> For any single program, the tool can build a statement-by-statement view:
										which lines only run when a particular condition is true, and which fields feed which other fields. Pick a
										program and press the button; it is computed in memory now and thrown away afterwards.
									</p>
									<p>
										<strong>Why it matters.</strong> This level of detail answers questions about <em>statements</em>, not about
										<em>domains</em>. Building it for every program in a 62-million-line estate would cost hundreds of millions of
										records and would not move a single boundary. So it is built only where it earns its keep: for a program
										the grouping could not place, or for a program about to be migrated. Doing it on demand here proves the
										machinery works without pretending it should run everywhere.
									</p>
									<p>
										<strong>What it found here.</strong> Whatever program you choose, the result lists its decision points and
										data flows with line numbers, and states plainly which COBOL constructs it does <em>not</em> model yet.
									</p>
									<div class="rt-actions">
										<select class="auto-profile" aria-label="Program for PDG" data-auto="dom.pdg-select" @onchange="OnPdgProgramChanged">
											@foreach (var candidate in CobolDomains.PdgCandidates())
											{
												<option value="@candidate" selected="@(candidate == _pdgProgram)">@candidate</option>
											}
										</select>
										<button class="primary-command compact" type="button" data-auto="dom.pdg" @onclick="BuildPdg">Look inside this program</button>
									</div>

									@if (_pdg is { } pdg)
									{
										<div class="compare-status" role="status" data-auto="dom.pdg-result">
											@pdg.Program &mdash; @pdg.Nodes.Count statement(s), @pdg.ControlEdges.Count depend on a condition,
											@pdg.DataEdges.Count field-to-field data flow(s), deepest nesting @pdg.MaxDepth.
										</div>
										<h4>Statements that only run when a condition holds (first 14)</h4>
										<table class="audit-table">
											<thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Paragraph</th><th>Statement</th><th>Runs only if</th><th>Line</th></tr></thead>
											<tbody>
												@foreach (var node in pdg.Nodes.Where(n => n.ControlParent >= 0).Take(14))
												{
													<tr>
														<td>@node.Ordinal</td><td><code>@node.Paragraph</code></td><td>@node.Verb</td>
														<td><small>@node.ControlCondition</small></td><td>@node.OriginalLine</td>
													</tr>
												}
											</tbody>
										</table>
										<h4>Which fields feed which (first 12)</h4>
										<table class="audit-table">
											<thead><tr><th>From</th><th>To</th><th>Through</th><th>Line</th></tr></thead>
											<tbody>
												@foreach (var edge in pdg.DataEdges.Take(12))
												{
													<tr><td><code>@edge.From</code></td><td><code>@edge.To</code></td><td>@edge.Via</td><td>@edge.OriginalLine</td></tr>
												}
											</tbody>
										</table>
										<h4>What this view does not yet model</h4>
										<ul class="rt-list warn">
											@foreach (var caveat in pdg.Caveats)
											{
												<li>@caveat</li>
											}
										</ul>
									}
								</div>
							</section>

							<section class="rt-box">
								<header class="rt-head"><span class="rt-step">7</span><div><strong>Read the programmers' notes</strong><small>pipeline stage S9 &middot; @domains.CommentReport.UsableBlocks of @domains.CommentReport.TotalBlocks comment blocks describe the code</small></div></header>
								<div class="audit-body">
									<p>
										<strong>What we did.</strong> Every comment block in the source is sorted into kinds: a program's header
										description, a note above a paragraph, an inline remark, a maintenance log entry (date, author, ticket,
										description), <em>old code that was commented out</em>, and licence or copyright boilerplate. Commented-out
										code is spotted by a simple trick: strip the comment marker and see if what is left parses as COBOL. If it
										does, it is dead code, not documentation.
									</p>
									<p>
										<strong>Why it matters.</strong> On our own estate the notes are known to be good &mdash; the 200-program
										pilot showed that. But notes have to be filtered before they are trusted. Dead code in comments carries the
										vocabulary of logic that no longer runs, and a copyright banner repeated in every program says nothing
										about the business. The maintenance logs are the hidden asset: <strong>programs that share a change-ticket
										number were changed together for a business reason</strong>, decades of it, which is an independent
										check on any grouping the tool proposes.
									</p>
									<p>
										<strong>What it found here.</strong> IBM's sample has @domains.CommentReport.Paragraph short notes above
										paragraphs and @domains.CommentReport.Inline inline remarks, but no program-level descriptions and no
										maintenance log; @domains.CommentReport.Boilerplate of @domains.CommentReport.TotalBlocks blocks are licence
										boilerplate and @domains.CommentReport.CommentedOutCode are commented-out code. That is too thin to name a
										domain from, so on this sample the notes contribute nothing to the domain names, and the tool says so
										instead of pretending. On the real estate this is expected to be one of the strongest signals available.
									</p>
									<table class="audit-table">
										<thead><tr><th>Program headers</th><th>Paragraph notes</th><th>Inline remarks</th><th>Maintenance log entries</th><th>Commented-out code</th><th>Boilerplate</th><th>Share that were duplicates</th></tr></thead>
										<tbody>
											<tr>
												<td>@domains.CommentReport.Header</td><td>@domains.CommentReport.Paragraph</td>
												<td>@domains.CommentReport.Inline</td><td>@domains.CommentReport.MaintenanceEntries</td>
												<td>@domains.CommentReport.CommentedOutCode</td><td>@domains.CommentReport.Boilerplate</td>
												<td>@domains.CommentReport.DedupRatio</td>
											</tr>
										</tbody>
									</table>
								</div>
							</section>

							<section class="rt-box" data-auto="dom.fields">
								<header class="rt-head"><span class="rt-step">8</span><div><strong>Turn cryptic field names into plain English</strong><small>pipeline stage S10 &middot; @domains.FieldCards.Count field(s) described &middot; @domains.FieldCards.Count(c => c.ColumnBinding is not null) tied to a database column</small></div></header>
								<div class="audit-body">
									<p>
										<strong>What we did.</strong> A COBOL field declaration such as <code>05 CUST-ID PIC X(10).</code> tells a
										program the size and shape of a field but tells a reader almost nothing. For every field in every copybook,
										the tool writes a short plain-English description &mdash; a &ldquo;card&rdquo; &mdash; assembled entirely
										from facts already in the source: the group the field sits in, the database column it maps to (from DB2's
										DCLGEN layouts), its named values, and its exact type and size.
									</p>
										<p>
										<strong>Why it matters.</strong> Later, people will search this estate by meaning &mdash; &ldquo;where is the
										vehicle identification number?&rdquo; A search engine given the raw declaration would group it with every
										other ten-character field in the estate, because it matches on <em>shape</em>, not on <em>subject</em>. The
										card gives the field words first, so the search finds what people mean. <strong>No AI model wrote these
										cards.</strong> Each one is built by rules, and each cites where its words came from.
									</p>
									<p>
										<strong>What it found here.</strong> @domains.FieldCards.Count fields were described;
										@domains.FieldCards.Count(c => c.ColumnBinding is not null) of them could be tied to a named column of a
										named table, which is where the richest wording comes from. Compare the left and right columns below.
									</p>
									<table class="audit-table">
										<thead><tr><th>Raw declaration (what the source says)</th><th>Plain-English card (what the tool writes, by rule)</th><th>Where the words came from</th></tr></thead>
										<tbody>
											@foreach (var card in domains.FieldCards.Where(c => c.ColumnBinding is not null).Take(6))
											{
												<tr>
													<td><code>@card.Declaration</code></td>
													<td>@card.CardText</td>
													<td><small>@string.Join("; ", card.Sources)</small></td>
												</tr>
											}
											@foreach (var card in domains.FieldCards.Where(c => c.ColumnBinding is null && c.GroupPath.Contains('>')).Take(3))
											{
												<tr>
													<td><code>@card.Declaration</code></td>
													<td>@card.CardText</td>
													<td><small>@string.Join("; ", card.Sources)</small></td>
												</tr>
											}
										</tbody>
									</table>
									<ul class="rt-list warn">
										<li><strong>A compiler gives us the shape, not the meaning.</strong> A real COBOL compiler tells us a field's exact position, length and number format &mdash; things you cannot reliably get by reading. It cannot tell us that <code>CUST-ID</code> means a customer. That has to come from somewhere that already holds a name.</li>
										<li><strong>DB2's DCLGEN layouts are the richest source of names.</strong> They pair each database column with the COBOL field that holds it, so <code>VIN-TEXT</code> stops being a code and becomes &ldquo;the VIN column of the inventory table&rdquo;.</li>
										<li><strong>The abbreviation glossary is hand-written for now.</strong> At scale it should be learned from the estate's own notes and then reviewed, because a wrong expansion is confident, wrong prose &mdash; worse than an opaque name.</li>
										<li><strong>Not yet used:</strong> the labels users see on screen. In a CICS estate the screen layouts attach the words a person actually reads to the field behind them. That is the strongest naming signal there is, and this sample's screen layouts were not included by IBM.</li>
									</ul>
								</div>
							</section>

							<section class="rt-box" data-auto="dom.neurons">
								<header class="rt-head"><span class="rt-step">9</span><div><strong>Group the programs into business domains</strong><small>pipeline stage S12 &middot; @domains.Neurons.Count proposed domain(s) &middot; @domains.BoundaryObjects.Count item(s) referred to a person</small></div></header>
								<div class="audit-body">
									<p>
										<strong>What we did.</strong> A standard community-detection method (Leiden) looks at the map from Step 4
										and finds groups of programs that are more connected to each other &mdash; through calls, shared
										copybooks and shared data &mdash; than to the rest. The method has a &ldquo;how fine&rdquo; dial. Rather
										than picking one setting and calling it the answer, the tool runs it at seven settings and asks, for every
										pair of programs, <em>how often did these two land in the same group?</em>
									</p>
									<p>
										<strong>Why it matters.</strong> A single run at a single setting is one opinion. Programs that stay
										together at every setting are a <strong>stable core</strong>: a domain you can defend. Programs that
										move between groups depending on the setting are <strong>boundary objects</strong>: genuinely shared
										things that belong to no single domain, or eroded seams. <strong>The tool does not force those into a
										group. It names them and refers them to a person who knows the business.</strong> That is the same rule
										used throughout this programme: when the evidence is ambiguous, say so rather than guess.
									</p>
									<p>
										<strong>What it found here.</strong> @domains.Neurons.Count group(s), which match the story in the introduction:
									</p>
									<ul class="rt-list">
										@foreach (var neuron in domains.Neurons)
										{
											<li><strong>@neuron.Label</strong> &mdash; @DomainGloss(neuron) Members: @string.Join(", ", neuron.Members.Select(m => m.Program)).</li>
										}
									</ul>
									<p>
										The batch loader <code>GAM0VDB</code> touches every table, so it is genuinely hard to place; the tool
										flags it for a person rather than deciding. The shared commarea copybook <code>GAM0BCA</code> is included by
										programs on both sides of the line &mdash; it is the contract between the two domains, and it is the file
										a careless repository split would duplicate. The <em>names</em> of the groups were taken from the database
										tables each group reads, because this sample's notes were too thin to name them (Step 7); a person turns
										&ldquo;Eastinvntry&rdquo; into &ldquo;Vehicle inventory&rdquo; in a minute. Naming is the cheap part;
										finding the seam with evidence is the expensive part.
									</p>

									<h4>Same map, seven &ldquo;how fine&rdquo; settings</h4>
									<table class="audit-table">
										<thead><tr><th>Setting (&gamma;)</th><th>Groups found</th><th>Quality score (modularity)</th></tr></thead>
										<tbody>
											@foreach (var run in domains.Sweep)
											{
												<tr><td>@run.Resolution</td><td>@run.Communities</td><td>@run.Modularity</td></tr>
											}
										</tbody>
									</table>
									<p class="rt-note">
										Read down the middle column: one group at coarse settings, two in the middle, three when pushed. The two-group
										answer is where the map naturally rests. The quality score is a mathematical measure, not a business argument;
										it is shown for completeness and is never what we rank domains by.
									</p>

									@foreach (var neuron in domains.Neurons)
									{
										<h4>Proposed domain @neuron.Id &mdash; @neuron.Label</h4>
										<p class="rt-note">Named from: @neuron.LabelSource. Data it is proven to write: @(neuron.OwnedArtifacts.Count == 0 ? "none provable in this sample (the loader's writes are built at run time)" : string.Join(", ", neuron.OwnedArtifacts)). Data it reads: @(neuron.ReadArtifacts.Count == 0 ? "none" : string.Join(", ", neuron.ReadArtifacts)).</p>
										<table class="audit-table">
											<thead><tr><th>Program</th><th>How often it stayed with this group (0&ndash;1)</th><th>Verdict</th></tr></thead>
											<tbody>
												@foreach (var member in neuron.Members)
												{
													<tr class="@(member.BoundaryObject ? "p0-req" : null)">
														<td><code>@member.Program</code></td><td>@member.Stability</td>
														<td>@(member.BoundaryObject ? "uncertain — referred to a person who knows the business" : "stable core")</td>
													</tr>
												}
											</tbody>
										</table>
									}

									@if (domains.BoundaryObjects.Count > 0)
									{
										<h4>Shared things the tool declined to assign</h4>
										<table class="audit-table">
											<thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Kind</th><th>Used by</th><th>Why it cannot be placed</th></tr></thead>
											<tbody>
												@foreach (var boundary in domains.BoundaryObjects)
												{
													<tr>
														<td><code>@boundary.Artifact</code></td><td>@boundary.Kind</td>
														<td>@string.Join(", ", boundary.TouchedBy)</td><td>@boundary.Why</td>
													</tr>
												}
											</tbody>
										</table>
									}

									@if (domains.Synapses.Count > 0)
									{
										<h4>What crosses the line between the domains &mdash; and what it would cost to cut</h4>
										<table class="audit-table">
											<thead><tr><th>Shared item</th><th>Kind of crossing</th><th>Who writes it</th><th>Who uses it</th><th>What cutting here costs</th></tr></thead>
											<tbody>
												@foreach (var synapse in domains.Synapses)
												{
													<tr>
														<td><code>@synapse.Artifact</code></td><td>@synapse.Direction</td>
														<td>@synapse.Writer</td><td>@string.Join(", ", synapse.Readers)</td><td>@synapse.Cost</td>
													</tr>
												}
											</tbody>
										</table>
										<p class="rt-note">
											On a real estate this table is the one leadership decides from. A crossing where one side only
											<em>reads</em> the other's data is cheap to cut: a feed or a view. A crossing where both sides
											<em>write</em> the same data is expensive: it becomes a synchronisation contract for the whole
											migration. Domains are ranked for migration by this cost, never by the quality score above.
										</p>
									}

									<ul class="rt-list warn">
										<li><strong>Five programs cannot show a real domain boundary.</strong> This step proves the grouping machinery runs, reports its uncertainty, and produces a sensible answer on a case a person can check by hand. The first result that could actually be <em>wrong</em> comes when it is run over the 200 programs our SMEs have already classified.</li>
									</ul>
								</div>
							</section>

							<section class="rt-box" data-auto="dom.cortex-box">
								<header class="rt-head"><span class="rt-step">10</span><div><strong>Ask the estate a question</strong><small>pipeline stage S13 &middot; @CobolDomains.EmbeddingStatus</small></div></header>
								<div class="audit-body">
									<p>
										<strong>What we did.</strong> Everything gathered above &mdash; programs, paragraphs, notes and the
										plain-English field cards &mdash; is made searchable. A question is answered three ways at once and the
										answers are merged: an exact word match (for names such as <code>GAM0VMM</code> or
										<code>CA-MAKE-SELECTED</code>, which people type verbatim), an optional meaning-based match (using a small
										local language model to find text that is <em>about</em> the same thing), and a direct look-up in the map
										for structural questions such as &ldquo;who reads MAKE_MODEL?&rdquo;.
									</p>
									<p>
										<strong>Why it matters.</strong> This is how the map becomes useful day to day &mdash; for an analyst, a
										developer, or an AI assistant that has been told to check before it guesses. A meaning-based search on its
										own is poor at exact program names; an exact-word search on its own cannot answer &ldquo;where is the
										price stored?&rdquo;; and a question like &ldquo;who writes this table?&rdquo; has a precise answer in the
										map that no search should approximate. Using all three, <strong>every answer cites the file and line it
										came from</strong>. An answer that cannot be cited is not an answer; it is a plausible sentence.
									</p>
									<p>
										<strong>Try it.</strong> Press <em>Search</em> with the question already filled in, then try
										&ldquo;who calls GAM0VMM&rdquo; or &ldquo;vehicle price&rdquo;. The meaning-based arm is switched on
										separately because it takes a few seconds of real computation the first time.
									</p>
									<div class="rt-actions">
										<input class="rt-code-input cortex-input" data-auto="dom.cortex-input" placeholder="who reads MAKE_MODEL" aria-label="Cortex query" @bind="_cortexQuery" @bind:event="oninput" />
										<button class="primary-command compact" type="button" data-auto="dom.cortex" @onclick="AskCortex">Search</button>
										<button class="secondary-command compact" type="button" data-auto="dom.warm" disabled="@_cortexWarming" @onclick="WarmCortex">@(CobolDomains.VectorsReady ? "Meaning-based search ready" : _cortexWarming ? "Preparing…" : "Enable meaning-based search")</button>
									</div>

									@if (_cortex is { } cortex)
									{
										@if (cortex.GraphAnswer is not null)
										{
											<div class="compare-status" role="status"><strong>Exact answer from the map:</strong> @cortex.GraphAnswer</div>
										}
										<p class="rt-note" data-auto="dom.cortex-result">How this was answered: @cortex.Method</p>
										<table class="audit-table">
											<thead><tr><th>Kind</th><th>Match</th><th>Word score</th><th>Meaning score</th><th>Map score</th><th>Combined</th><th>Domain</th><th>Evidence (file:line)</th></tr></thead>
											<tbody>
												@foreach (var hit in cortex.Hits)
												{
													<tr>
														<td>@hit.Kind</td><td><code>@hit.Title</code></td>
														<td>@hit.LexicalScore</td><td>@hit.VectorScore</td><td>@hit.GraphScore</td>
														<td><strong>@hit.Fused</strong></td><td>@hit.Neuron</td>
														<td><small>@hit.Member:@hit.Line</small></td>
													</tr>
												}
											</tbody>
										</table>
									}
								</div>
							</section>

							<section class="rt-box">
								<header class="rt-head"><span class="rt-step">11</span><div><strong>Publish the map as reviewable knowledge</strong><small>pipeline stage S14 &middot; @domains.Okf.Count file(s) generated</small></div></header>
								<div class="audit-body">
									<p>
										<strong>What we did.</strong> The result is written out as a small set of plain-text files in the Open
										Knowledge Format (OKF) used across this programme: one file per proposed domain, one per shared item the
										tool could not place, and an index that repeats the coverage numbers from Step 1 at the top.
									</p>
									<p>
										<strong>Why it matters.</strong> A map in a database is invisible; a map in reviewable text files can be
										read by a person, diffed in source control, and handed to an AI assistant as trusted context. Each file
										says who generated it and when, and carries <code>verified: null</code> &mdash; meaning <strong>no
										subject-matter expert has signed this off yet</strong>. Sign-off is a human act, recorded in the file.
										When the pipeline is re-run, the generated files are regenerated and compared; a difference nobody
										expected fails the build. That is how the map is kept from going quietly stale.
									</p>
									<p>
										<strong>What it found here.</strong> Below are the first files as generated. Notice the coverage line in the
										index and the <code>verified: null</code> on every file.
									</p>
									@foreach (var file in domains.Okf.Take(4))
									{
										<h4>@file.Path</h4>
										<pre class="rt-code">---
@file.Frontmatter
---
@file.Body</pre>
									}
								</div>
							</section>

							<section class="rt-qa" data-auto="dom.findings">
								<header class="rt-head"><span class="rt-step">&#10003;</span><div><strong>What this run found, in plain English</strong><small>every sentence below was produced from the evidence above, not written in advance</small></div></header>
								<ul class="rt-list">
									@foreach (var finding in domains.Findings)
									{
										<li>@finding</li>
									}
								</ul>

								<h4>The tool checked itself</h4>
								<p class="rt-note">
									Every stage runs a check on its own output and prints the result. A broken step is visible here before anyone
									presents it. &ldquo;Attention&rdquo; means a step found something worth a look, not that it failed.
								</p>
								<table class="audit-table">
									<thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>What was checked</th><th>Result</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead>
									<tbody>
										@foreach (var check in domains.Checks)
										{
											<tr>
												<td>@check.Stage</td><td>@check.Name</td>
												<td>@(check.Passed ? "pass" : "attention")</td><td><small>@check.Detail</small></td>
											</tr>
										}
									</tbody>
								</table>

								<h4>What this does and does not prove</h4>
								<ul class="rt-list warn">
									<li><strong>It proves the machinery.</strong> Real IBM COBOL, real copybooks, real JCL and real CICS definitions were read end to end by the industry-standard COBOL grammar, and every fact on this page can be traced to a file and a line. That is the part that has to work before anything else can.</li>
									<li><strong>It does not prove a domain boundary.</strong> Five programs are one small application. The two groups found here are correct and checkable by hand, but the first result that could actually be wrong &mdash; and therefore the first real test &mdash; is the run over the 200 programs our own SMEs have already classified.</li>
									<li><strong>A proposed domain is a hypothesis.</strong> Sign-off is a human act. The tool's job is to make the hypothesis cheap to test and its evidence easy to check, and to say clearly where it is unsure.</li>
									<li><strong>The coverage numbers are part of the result.</strong> On a real estate some programs will not parse, some copybooks will be missing, and some SQL will be built at run time. The map will be presented as a claim about the part of the estate that was read, with the percentages attached, every time.</li>
									<li><strong>This sample's notes were thin</strong> (mostly IBM licence text and one-line paragraph labels). Our own estate's notes are known to be good, which is why the pipeline treats them as a first-class signal &mdash; and why their accuracy will be measured on a sample before they are trusted at scale.</li>
									<li><strong>Repositories should be split only after the map is agreed</strong>, one domain at a time, as it is migrated. A wrong line on the map costs an edit; a wrong repository split costs severed history and duplicated copybooks that drift apart silently.</li>
									<li>The sample is the IBM Global Auto Mart demonstration, used under its as-is sample licence with copyright notices intact. The full design is in <code>migrateCOBOLprojectToJAVA.md</code>.</li>
								</ul>
							</section>
						}
					</section>
					break;
				case 18:
					<section>
						<p class="kicker">STOP BAD WORK BEFORE IT STARTS</p>
						<h2 id="scene-title">A ticket an agent cannot act on</h2>
						<p class="rt-intro">Most agent failures are underspecified tickets, not weak models. A <strong>deterministic pre-check</strong> scores the ticket before any model sees it. Claude then grades it against the same rubric and rewrites it so it would pass.</p>

						@if (_session?.TicketGate is { } gate)
						{
							<section class="rt-box rt-input-box">
								<header class="rt-head"><span class="rt-step">1</span><div><strong>The ticket</strong><small>edit it and re-run to see the score move</small></div></header>
								<textarea class="rt-code-input" spellcheck="false" maxlength="8000" @bind="_ticketText" @bind:event="oninput" disabled="@TicketBusy" aria-label="JIRA ticket"></textarea>
								<div class="rt-actions">
									<small>@_ticketText.Length / 8000</small>
									<button class="primary-command compact" type="button" data-auto="s19.run" disabled="@(!_bridgeConnected || TicketBusy)" @onclick="RunTicketGate">@TicketButtonText</button>
								</div>
								@if (!_bridgeConnected)
								{
									<p class="bridge-required" role="status">Connect the VSIX with the token shown under Local Bridge before running the gate.</p>
								}
							</section>

							@if (LivePrecheck is { } precheck)
							{
								<section class="rt-box">
									<header class="rt-head"><span class="rt-step">2</span><div><strong>Deterministic pre-check</strong><small>no model, no bridge · recomputed as you type</small></div></header>
									<div class="@(precheck.Passed ? "compare-status" : "compare-status failed")" role="status">
										Score @precheck.Score / 100 — @(precheck.Passed ? "PASSES the gate" : "FAILS the gate (needs 70)")
									</div>
									<table class="audit-table">
										<thead><tr><th>Criterion</th><th>Met</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead>
										<tbody>
											@foreach (var signal in precheck.Signals)
											{
												<tr><td>@signal.Name</td><td>@(signal.Present ? "yes" : "no")</td><td>@signal.Detail</td></tr>
											}
										</tbody>
									</table>
									@if (precheck.VagueTerms.Count > 0)
									{
										<h4>Vague language found</h4>
										<ul class="rt-list warn">@foreach (var term in precheck.VagueTerms) { <li>“@term”</li> }</ul>
									}
								</section>
							}

							<section class="rt-qa">
								<header class="rt-head"><span class="rt-step">3</span><div><strong>Review and rewrite</strong><small>@(gate.Review?.Model ?? "Claude Opus 5")</small></div></header>
								@if (gate.Review is { } graded)
								{
									<div class="@(graded.Passed ? "compare-status" : "compare-status failed")" role="status">
										Model score @graded.Score / 100 — @(graded.Passed ? "would pass" : "would not pass")
									</div>
									<p class="rt-reco">@graded.Verdict</p>
									<table class="audit-table">
										<thead><tr><th>Criterion</th><th>Met</th><th>Note</th></tr></thead>
										<tbody>
											@foreach (var finding in graded.Findings)
											{
												<tr><td>@finding.Name</td><td>@(finding.Present ? "yes" : "no")</td><td>@finding.Detail</td></tr>
											}
										</tbody>
									</table>
									@if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(graded.Rewritten))
									{
										<h4>The same ticket, rewritten to pass</h4>
										<pre class="rt-story">@graded.Rewritten</pre>
									}
								}
								else if (TicketBusy)
								{
									<div class="rt-pending"><span></span>Claude is grading the ticket…</div>
								}
								else if (gate.Status == "failed")
								{
									<div class="compare-status failed" role="alert">@(gate.Error ?? "The review did not complete.")</div>
								}
								else
								{
									<div class="rt-placeholder">The review and rewrite will appear here.</div>
								}
							</section>
						}
					</section>
					break;
				case 19:
					<section>
						<p class="kicker">FIND WHAT WAS PLANTED</p>
						<h2 id="scene-title">Auditing a cloud design an AI helped write</h2>
						<p class="rt-intro">A proposed GCP sandbox with defects deliberately planted in it. Gemma audits it against the five-pillar checklist; Claude then grades that audit against the sealed list of what was actually planted. The list is revealed only after grading.</p>

						@if (_session?.TrapHunt is { } hunt)
						{
							<section class="rt-box">
								<header class="rt-head"><span class="rt-step">1</span><div><strong>The proposed design</strong><small>Terraform · human + LLM authored</small></div></header>
								<pre class="rt-code">@hunt.Design</pre>
								<div class="rt-actions">
									<small>@GovernanceSamples.PlantedTraps.Length defects are planted in this file</small>
									<button class="primary-command compact" type="button" data-auto="s20.run" disabled="@(!_bridgeConnected || TrapBusy)" @onclick="RunTrapHunt">@TrapButtonText</button>
								</div>
								@if (!_bridgeConnected)
								{
									<p class="bridge-required" role="status">Connect the VSIX with the token shown under Local Bridge before running the audit.</p>
								}
							</section>

							<section class="rt-box">
								<header class="rt-head"><span class="rt-step">2</span><div><strong>The audit</strong><small>@(hunt.FindingsModel ?? "Gemma 4")</small></div></header>
								@if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(hunt.Findings))
								{
									<pre class="rt-story">@hunt.Findings</pre>
								}
								else if (hunt.Status == "hunting")
								{
									<div class="rt-pending"><span></span>Gemma is auditing the design…</div>
								}
								else
								{
									<div class="rt-placeholder">The agent's findings will appear here.</div>
								}
							</section>

							<section class="rt-qa">
								<header class="rt-head"><span class="rt-step">3</span><div><strong>Graded against the sealed list</strong><small>@(hunt.JudgeModel ?? "Claude Opus 5")</small></div></header>
								@if (hunt.Grade is { } grade)
								{
									<div class="@(grade.TrapsFound == grade.TrapsTotal ? "compare-status" : "compare-status failed")" role="status">
										Found @grade.TrapsFound of @grade.TrapsTotal planted defects.
									</div>
									<p class="rt-reco">@grade.Verdict</p>
									@if (grade.Matched.Count > 0)
									{
										<h4>Caught</h4><ul class="rt-list good">@foreach (var item in grade.Matched) { <li>@item</li> }</ul>
									}
									@if (grade.Missed.Count > 0)
									{
										<h4>Missed</h4><ul class="rt-list warn">@foreach (var item in grade.Missed) { <li>@item</li> }</ul>
									}
									@if (grade.FalsePositives.Count > 0)
									{
										<h4>False positives — these cost a reviewer's time</h4>
										<ul class="rt-list warn">@foreach (var item in grade.FalsePositives) { <li>@item</li> }</ul>
									}
									@if (hunt.PlantedTraps.Count > 0)
									{
										<h4>What was planted</h4>
										<ol class="rt-list">@foreach (var item in hunt.PlantedTraps) { <li>@item</li> }</ol>
									}
								}
								else if (hunt.Status == "judging")
								{
									<div class="rt-pending"><span></span>Claude is grading the audit…</div>
								}
								else if (hunt.Status == "failed")
								{
									<div class="compare-status failed" role="alert">@(hunt.Error ?? "The audit did not complete.")</div>
								}
								else
								{
									<div class="rt-placeholder">The grade will appear here once the audit completes.</div>
								}
							</section>
						}
					</section>
					break;
				case 20:
					<section>
						<p class="kicker">IS THE AGENT STILL DOING THE JOB?</p>
						<h2 id="scene-title">One golden case, three layers of drift</h2>
						<p class="rt-intro">A frozen commit, the ticket, and the patch a competent human wrote. A candidate patch is scored across three layers: <strong>functional</strong>, <strong>structural</strong> and <strong>semantic</strong>. The first two are deterministic. Only the last asks a model — and only about intent.</p>

						@if (_session?.Drift is { } drift)
						{
							<section class="rt-box">
								<header class="rt-head"><span class="rt-step">1</span><div><strong>The golden case</strong><small>ticket + frozen commit + the human patch</small></div></header>
								<pre class="rt-story">@drift.Ticket</pre>
								<div class="rt-flow rt-flow-stacked">
									<div><h4>Frozen baseline</h4><pre class="rt-code">@drift.FrozenCode</pre></div>
									<div><h4>Human patch</h4><pre class="rt-code">@drift.HumanPatch</pre></div>
								</div>
							</section>

							<section class="rt-box rt-input-box">
								<header class="rt-head"><span class="rt-step">2</span><div><strong>Candidate patch</strong><small>@drift.CandidateLabel</small></div></header>
								<pre class="rt-code">@drift.CandidatePatch</pre>
								<div class="rt-actions">
									<small>Score the drifted patch, or the human patch as a control</small>
									<div>
										<button class="secondary-command compact" type="button" data-auto="s21.control" disabled="@(!_bridgeConnected || DriftBusy)" @onclick="() => RunDrift(true)">Score the control</button>
										<button class="primary-command compact" type="button" data-auto="s21.agent" disabled="@(!_bridgeConnected || DriftBusy)" @onclick="() => RunDrift(false)">@(DriftBusy ? "Scoring…" : "Score the agent patch")</button>
									</div>
								</div>
								@if (!_bridgeConnected)
								{
									<p class="bridge-required" role="status">The structural layer below works without the bridge. Connect the VSIX to add the semantic layer.</p>
								}
							</section>

							@if (drift.Structural is { } structural)
							{
								<section class="rt-box">
									<header class="rt-head"><span class="rt-step">3</span><div><strong>Structural layer</strong><small>deterministic · diffed against the human patch</small></div></header>
									<div class="@(structural.Score == 100 ? "compare-status" : "compare-status failed")" role="status">
										Structural score @structural.Score / 100
									</div>
									<table class="audit-table">
										<thead><tr><th>Check</th><th>Held</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead>
										<tbody>
											@foreach (var finding in structural.Findings)
											{
												<tr><td>@finding.Name</td><td>@(finding.Present ? "yes" : "no")</td><td>@finding.Detail</td></tr>
											}
										</tbody>
									</table>
									<h4>Functional layer — the verification oracle</h4>
									<ul class="rt-list">@foreach (var check in DriftSamples.Oracle) { <li>@check.Name — <code>@check.Assertion</code></li> }</ul>
								</section>
							}

							<section class="rt-qa">
								<header class="rt-head"><span class="rt-step">4</span><div><strong>Semantic layer</strong><small>@(drift.Semantic?.Model ?? "Claude Opus 5") · goal fidelity and scope discipline</small></div></header>
								@if (drift.Semantic is { } semantic)
								{
									<div class="clara-signature">
										Goal fidelity <strong>@semantic.GoalFidelity/5</strong> · scope discipline <strong>@semantic.ScopeDiscipline/5</strong>
									</div>
									<p class="rt-reco">@semantic.Verdict</p>
									@if (semantic.Deviations.Count > 0)
									{
										<h4>Departures from the ticket</h4>
