				case 34:
					<section>
						<p class="kicker">SEND THE MODEL THE PAGES ABOUT ITS TASK &mdash; AND PROVE NOTHING WAS LOST</p>
						<h2 id="scene-title">Classify Context</h2>
						<p class="rt-intro">
							In a long working session people change subject. Every later question then pays for every earlier
							paragraph, and the model has to find the relevant part in the noise. This scene tests a cheap, local fix:
							a <strong>classifier that runs on this machine</strong> reads a design document that mixes two unrelated
							services, decides section by section which ones the implementer needs, and drops the rest
							<strong>whole</strong> &mdash; nothing that is kept is altered, so every kept sentence can still be quoted.
						</p>
						<p class="rt-intro">
							Then the real test: <strong>Gemma 4 builds the service three times</strong> &mdash; from everything, from
							what the classifier kept, and from what Claude kept &mdash; and each build is compiled and run against a
							reference answer. If the filtered builds pass and the full build cost more, the saving is real. If a
							filtered build fails a case the full build passed, the filter threw away something that mattered, and the
							page shows exactly which section.
						</p>

						@{
							var ccFixed = Classifier.DomainDocument(ClassifyContextSamples.FixedIncome);
							var ccCrypto = Classifier.DomainDocument(ClassifyContextSamples.Crypto);
							var ccShared = Classifier.DomainDocument(ClassifyContextSamples.Shared);
							var ccMixed = Classifier.MixedDocument();
							var ccMixedTokens = Classifier.Tokens(ContextClassifierService.ToText(ccMixed));
							var ccIdealTokens = Classifier.Tokens(ContextClassifierService.ToText(Classifier.IdealKeptSet()));
							var cc = _session?.ClassifyContext;
						}

						<section class="rt-box" data-auto="cc.designs">
							<header class="rt-head"><span class="rt-step">1</span><div><strong>Two designs, written separately</strong><small>@ccFixed.Length fixed-income sections &middot; @ccCrypto.Length crypto sections &middot; @ccShared.Length shared platform sections</small></div></header>
							<div class="audit-body">
								<p>
									<strong>What this is.</strong> A design document for two new services on the trading platform. The
									<em>bond settlement amount service</em> belongs to the fixed-income desk and is the one we will ask
									Gemma to build. The <em>wallet screening and instant settlement service</em> belongs to the crypto desk
									and is the noise. A third group of sections &mdash; record format, rounding, logging, testing,
									deployment &mdash; applies to both and is labelled <em>shared</em>.
								</p>
								<p>
									<strong>Why it is set up this way.</strong> Because the two designs were authored separately and
									tagged, the right answer for every section is known in advance without anyone hand-labelling
									anything. Two fixed-income sections deliberately depend on crypto-labelled sections &mdash; the
									rejection code table and the settlement cycle live over there. Those are the <strong>traps</strong>:
									a filter that drops everything it does not recognise will lose them, and the compiled result will show it.
								</p>
								<div class="rt-flow">
									<div>
										<h4>Fixed income &mdash; bond settlement amount</h4>
										@foreach (var segment in ccFixed)
										{
											<details class="cc-seg cc-fi"><summary><code>@segment.Id</code> @segment.Title</summary><p>@segment.Body</p></details>
										}
									</div>
									<div>
										<h4>Crypto &mdash; wallet screening and instant settlement</h4>
										@foreach (var segment in ccCrypto)
										{
											<details class="cc-seg cc-cr"><summary><code>@segment.Id</code> @segment.Title</summary><p>@segment.Body</p></details>
										}
									</div>
								</div>
								<h4>Shared platform conventions (both services depend on these)</h4>
								@foreach (var segment in ccShared)
								{
									<details class="cc-seg cc-sh"><summary><code>@segment.Id</code> @segment.Title</summary><p>@segment.Body</p></details>
								}
								<p class="rt-note">
									Trap sections: <code>FI-MARGIN</code> refers to the reason-code table in <code>CR-CODES</code>;
									<code>FI-SETTLE</code> refers to the settlement cycle in <code>CR-LIFECYCLE</code>. Both are marked in the
									text with a &sect; reference, exactly as a real design document would do it.
								</p>
							</div>
						</section>

						<section class="rt-box" data-auto="cc.mixed">
							<header class="rt-head"><span class="rt-step">2</span><div><strong>The mixed document &mdash; what a model would actually receive</strong><small>@ccMixed.Length sections &middot; @ccMixedTokens.ToString("N0") tokens &middot; shuffled with a fixed seed</small></div></header>
							<div class="audit-body">
								<p>
									<strong>What we did.</strong> The sections were shuffled together with a fixed seed (so the result is
									the same every run), renumbered 1 to @ccMixed.Length, and every cross-reference was rewritten to the new
									number. The labels travel with each section as sealed ground truth but are <strong>not</strong> in the
									text the classifier or the models see. This stands in for a chat history that has drifted across topics.
								</p>
								<div class="rt-actions">
									<small>@(_ccReveal ? "Sealed labels shown for the audience. The classifier never sees them." : "Labels hidden — this is exactly what the classifier sees.")</small>
									<button class="secondary-command compact" type="button" @onclick="() => _ccReveal = !_ccReveal">@(_ccReveal ? "Hide sealed labels" : "Reveal sealed labels")</button>
								</div>
								<table class="audit-table">
									<thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Section</th>@if (_ccReveal) { <th>Sealed label</th> }<th>Opening words</th></tr></thead>
									<tbody>
										@foreach (var segment in ccMixed)
										{
											<tr class="@(_ccReveal ? DomainClass(segment.SealedDomain) : null)">
												<td>@segment.Number</td>
												<td>@segment.Title</td>
												@if (_ccReveal) { <td>@segment.SealedDomain</td> }
												<td><small>@(segment.Body.Length > 140 ? segment.Body[..140] + "…" : segment.Body)</small></td>
											</tr>
										}
									</tbody>
								</table>
								<p class="rt-note">
									A perfect filter for the bond service would keep @Classifier.IdealKeptSet().Length of @ccMixed.Length sections
									(@ccIdealTokens.ToString("N0") tokens &mdash; @(ccMixedTokens == 0 ? 0 : 100 - (int)Math.Round(100.0 * ccIdealTokens / ccMixedTokens))% less than the mixed document). That is the floor the filters below are measured against.
								</p>
							</div>
						</section>

						<section class="rt-box" data-auto="cc.classify-box">
							<header class="rt-head"><span class="rt-step">3</span><div><strong>Arm B &mdash; separate the two with embeddinggemma + ML.NET</strong><small>runs here, no model call, no bridge</small></div></header>
							<div class="audit-body">
								<p>
									<strong>What we did.</strong> Each section is turned into a vector by embeddinggemma-300m (a 300-million
									parameter local model, the same one the other scenes use) and classified by an ML.NET model that was
									trained on the two real trading repositories: the fixed-income code and notes as one class, the crypto
									code and notes as another, and the framework code the two repositories share as the third,
									&ldquo;shared&rdquo; class. A second, purely lexical vote uses each repository's own distinctive vocabulary.
									<strong>The design document was never used in training.</strong>
								</p>
								<p>
									<strong>Two rules keep it safe.</strong> First, the decision is <em>asymmetric</em>: a section the classifier
									is unsure about is kept, because dropping a real requirement is far worse than keeping a stray paragraph.
									Second, if a kept section says &ldquo;see &sect;14&rdquo;, section 14 is kept too, whatever the classifier
									thought of it. That second rule is what catches the traps.
								</p>
								<p class="rt-note">@Classifier.Training.Method Training set: @Classifier.Training.TrainingDocuments documents (@Classifier.Training.FixedIncomeDocuments fixed income, @Classifier.Training.CryptoDocuments crypto, @Classifier.Training.SharedDocuments shared); vocabulary @Classifier.Training.FixedIncomeTerms fixed-income and @Classifier.Training.CryptoTerms crypto terms.</p>
								<div class="rt-actions">
									<small>Deterministic for a given model file. Same input, same answer.</small>
									<button class="primary-command compact" type="button" data-auto="cc.classify" disabled="@(_session is null)" @onclick="RunContextClassifier">@(cc?.Classifier is null ? "Separate the document" : "Run again")</button>
								</div>

								@if (cc?.Classifier is { } filter)
								{
									<div class="audit-scoreline" data-auto="cc.classify-result">
										<span class="ctx-label @(filter.FixedIncomeKept == filter.FixedIncomeTotal && filter.TrapsHonoured == filter.TrapsTotal ? "ctx-rich" : "ctx-poor")">@filter.FixedIncomeKept/@filter.FixedIncomeTotal</span>
										<div>
											<strong>Fixed-income sections kept</strong>
											<small>@filter.CryptoDropped of @filter.CryptoTotal crypto sections dropped &middot; @filter.SharedKept of @filter.SharedTotal shared kept &middot; @filter.Uncertain kept as uncertain &middot; @filter.KeptByReference kept by reference &middot; traps honoured @filter.TrapsHonoured/@filter.TrapsTotal</small>
										</div>
									</div>
									<p class="rt-reco">
										Tokens: @ccMixedTokens.ToString("N0") in the mixed document &rarr; <strong>@filter.PromptTokens.ToString("N0")</strong> kept
										(<strong>@(ccMixedTokens == 0 ? 0 : 100 - (int)Math.Round(100.0 * filter.PromptTokens / ccMixedTokens))% less</strong>; a perfect filter would reach @ccIdealTokens.ToString("N0")).
										@(filter.FixedIncomeKept < filter.FixedIncomeTotal ? "A fixed-income section was dropped — that is the failure this experiment exists to catch, and Step 5 will show its cost." : "Nothing the bond service needs was dropped.")
									</p>
									<table class="audit-table">
										<thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Section</th><th>Sealed</th><th>Classifier said</th><th>FI</th><th>Crypto</th><th>Shared</th><th>Margin</th><th>Decision</th></tr></thead>
										<tbody>
											@foreach (var ccd in filter.Decisions)
											{
												<tr class="@DecisionClass(ccd)">
													<td>@ccd.Number</td><td>@ccd.Title</td>
													<td>@ccd.SealedDomain</td><td>@ccd.Predicted</td>
													<td>@ccd.FixedIncome</td><td>@ccd.Crypto</td><td>@ccd.Shared</td><td>@ccd.Margin</td>
													<td>@(ccd.Kept ? "KEEP" : "drop") &mdash; <small>@ccd.Reason</small></td>
												</tr>
											}
										</tbody>
									</table>
									<p class="rt-note">
										Highlighted rows are where the decision disagrees with the sealed label. A crypto section kept because a
										fixed-income section refers to it is <em>not</em> a mistake &mdash; it is the trap being handled correctly.
									</p>
								}
							</div>
						</section>

						<section class="rt-box" data-auto="cc.claude-box">
							<header class="rt-head"><span class="rt-step">4</span><div><strong>Arm D &mdash; let Claude Opus 5 do the filtering</strong><small>the expensive way to do the same job &middot; @(cc?.ClaudeFilterPromptTokens > 0 ? $"{cc.ClaudeFilterPromptTokens:N0} tokens billed to filter" : "for comparison")</small></div></header>
							<div class="audit-body">
								<p>
									<strong>What we did.</strong> Claude is given the same mixed document and the same instruction &mdash;
									keep what the bond service implementer needs, follow references, drop the rest &mdash; and returns a
									keep-list. It is scored by exactly the same rules as the classifier. This is the natural alternative:
									instead of a local classifier, just ask the big model to prune. It works; the question is what it
									costs, every time, on every turn.
								</p>
								<div class="rt-actions">
									<small>Requires the VSIX bridge. Claude reads the whole document, so this call is billed at full length.</small>
									<button class="primary-command compact" type="button" data-auto="cc.claude" disabled="@(!_bridgeConnected || cc?.Classifier is null || ClassifyBusy)" @onclick="RunContextClaudeFilter">@ClaudeFilterButtonText</button>
								</div>
								@if (!_bridgeConnected)
								{
									<p class="bridge-required" role="status">Connect the VSIX with the token shown under Local Bridge to run Steps 4 and 5.</p>
								}
								@if (cc?.Status == "claude-filtering")
								{
									<div class="rt-pending cc-progress" role="status" aria-live="polite"><span></span><div><strong>Claude is reading the mixed document and choosing sections…</strong><small>@ClassifyElapsed elapsed · one model call, billed at the full document length</small></div></div>
								}
								@if (cc?.ClaudeFilter is { } claude)
								{
									<div class="audit-scoreline" data-auto="cc.claude-result">
										<span class="ctx-label @(claude.FixedIncomeKept == claude.FixedIncomeTotal && claude.TrapsHonoured == claude.TrapsTotal ? "ctx-rich" : "ctx-poor")">@claude.FixedIncomeKept/@claude.FixedIncomeTotal</span>
										<div>
											<strong>Fixed-income sections kept by @cc.ClaudeFilterModel</strong>
											<small>@claude.CryptoDropped of @claude.CryptoTotal crypto dropped &middot; @claude.SharedKept of @claude.SharedTotal shared kept &middot; traps honoured @claude.TrapsHonoured/@claude.TrapsTotal &middot; @claude.PromptTokens.ToString("N0") tokens kept for the implementer</small>
										</div>
									</div>
									@if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(claude.Note))
									{
										<p class="rt-reco">Claude's own note: @claude.Note</p>
									}
									<table class="audit-table">
										<thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Section</th><th>Sealed</th><th>Claude</th><th>Classifier</th><th>Claude's reason</th></tr></thead>
										<tbody>
											@foreach (var ccd in claude.Decisions)
											{
												var local = cc.Classifier?.Decisions.FirstOrDefault(x => x.Number == ccd.Number);
												<tr class="@DecisionClass(ccd)">
													<td>@ccd.Number</td><td>@ccd.Title</td><td>@ccd.SealedDomain</td>
													<td>@(ccd.Kept ? "KEEP" : "drop")</td>
													<td>@(local is null ? "—" : local.Kept ? "KEEP" : "drop")</td>
													<td><small>@ccd.Reason</small></td>
												</tr>
											}
										</tbody>
									</table>
								}
							</div>
						</section>

						<section class="rt-box" data-auto="cc.implement-box">
							<header class="rt-head"><span class="rt-step">5</span><div><strong>Gemma 4 builds the service from each version, and the code is executed</strong><small>compiled and run against a reference answer &middot; no model grades this</small></div></header>
							<div class="audit-body">
								<p>
									<strong>What we did.</strong> The same Gemma 4, the same instruction, three different amounts of context:
									<strong>A</strong> the whole mixed document, <strong>B</strong> what the classifier kept, <strong>D</strong> what
									Claude kept. Each answer is compiled in a sandbox and run against @ClassifyContextSamples.TestVectors.Length
									input records whose correct output was computed by a reference implementation of the design &mdash;
									including the buying-power rejection and the settlement cycle that depend on the trap sections. The code is
									also scanned for crypto vocabulary that has no business in a bond service.
								</p>
								<p>
									<strong>How to read it.</strong> Tokens down and cases equal is a saving. A case that A passes and B or D fails
									names the section the filter should not have dropped. Leaked terms show whether the noise in A became wrong
									code rather than just cost.
								</p>
								<div class="rt-actions">
									<small>@(cc?.ClaudeFilter is null ? "Runs Claude's filter first (Step 4), then builds arms A, B and D." : "Builds arms A, B and D.") One run each; the models are not deterministic.</small>
									<button class="primary-command compact" type="button" data-auto="cc.implement" disabled="@(!_bridgeConnected || cc?.Classifier is null || ClassifyBusy)" @onclick="RunContextImplementations">@ImplementButtonText</button>
								</div>
								@if (ClassifyBusy)
								{
									<div class="rt-pending cc-progress" role="status" aria-live="polite" data-auto="cc.progress">
										<span></span>
										<div>
											<strong>@ClassifyProgressHeadline</strong>
											<small>@ClassifyProgressDetail</small>
										</div>
									</div>
								}

								@if (cc?.Arms.Count > 0)
								{
									<table class="audit-table" data-auto="cc.results">
										<thead><tr><th>Arm</th><th>Context given</th><th>Prompt tokens</th><th>vs A</th><th>Output tokens</th><th>Compiled</th><th>Cases passed</th><th>Crypto terms in code</th><th>Status</th></tr></thead>
										<tbody>
											@{ var armA = cc.Arms.FirstOrDefault(a => a.Arm == "A"); }
											@foreach (var arm in cc.Arms)
											{
												<tr class="@(arm.Status == "completed" && arm.Passed == ClassifyContextSamples.TestVectors.Length ? "p0-opt" : arm.Status is "completed" or "failed" ? "p0-req" : null)">
													<td><strong>@arm.Arm</strong></td><td>@arm.Label</td>
													<td>@arm.PromptTokens.ToString("N0")</td>
													<td>@(armA is null || armA.PromptTokens == 0 || arm.Arm == "A" ? "—" : $"{100 - (int)Math.Round(100.0 * arm.PromptTokens / armA.PromptTokens)}% less")</td>
													<td>@(arm.Status == "completed" ? arm.OutputTokens.ToString("N0") : "—")</td>
													<td>@(arm.Status == "completed" ? (arm.Compiled ? "yes" : "NO") : "—")</td>
													<td>@(arm.Status == "completed" && arm.Compiled ? $"{arm.Passed} / {ClassifyContextSamples.TestVectors.Length}" : "—")</td>
													<td>@(arm.Status == "completed" ? (arm.LeakedTerms.Count == 0 ? "none" : string.Join(", ", arm.LeakedTerms)) : "—")</td>
													<td>@(arm.Status == "pending" ? "queued — waiting for the bridge" : arm.Status == "running" ? "Gemma is writing the code…" : arm.Status == "failed" ? arm.Error : arm.ModelUsed)</td>
												</tr>
											}
										</tbody>
									</table>

									@if (armA is not null && cc.Arms.Count > 1)
									{
										<h4>The saving, net of what it cost to filter</h4>
										<table class="audit-table">
											<thead><tr><th>Arm</th><th>Implementer tokens saved vs A</th><th>Tokens spent to produce the filter</th><th>Which model spent them</th><th>Net</th></tr></thead>
											<tbody>
												@foreach (var arm in cc.Arms.Where(a => a.Arm != "A"))
												{
													var saved = armA.PromptTokens - arm.PromptTokens;
													var filterCost = arm.Arm == "D" ? cc.ClaudeFilterPromptTokens : 0;
													<tr class="@(saved - filterCost > 0 ? "p0-opt" : "p0-req")">
														<td><strong>@arm.Arm</strong></td>
														<td>@saved.ToString("N0")</td>
														<td>@filterCost.ToString("N0")</td>
														<td>@(arm.Arm == "D" ? $"{cc.ClaudeFilterModel} — reads the whole document every time" : "none — the classifier runs locally, no model call")</td>
														<td>@((saved - filterCost).ToString("N0")) tokens @(saved - filterCost > 0 ? "saved" : "spent")</td>
													</tr>
												}
											</tbody>
										</table>
										<p class="rt-note">
											A filter that reads the whole document to decide what to drop has already paid for the whole document. It can
											only pay off if the filtered result is reused across many later turns, and only if the model doing the reading
											is cheaper than the model being spared. The classifier's column is zero because it is a local model with no
											per-token cost — that, not the percentage, is the point.
										</p>
									}

									@foreach (var arm in cc.Arms.Where(a => a.Status == "completed"))
									{
										<details class="cc-arm">
											<summary><strong>Arm @arm.Arm</strong> &mdash; @arm.Label: @(arm.Compiled ? $"{arm.Passed} of {ClassifyContextSamples.TestVectors.Length} cases match the reference" : "did not compile")</summary>
											@if (!arm.Compiled)
											{
												<div class="compare-status failed" role="alert">@arm.Error</div>
												<ul class="rt-list warn">@foreach (var error in arm.CompileErrors) { <li>@error</li> }</ul>
											}
											@if (arm.Cases.Count > 0)
											{
												<table class="audit-table">
													<thead><tr><th>Input record</th><th>Reference says</th><th>This build says</th><th></th></tr></thead>
													<tbody>
														@foreach (var item in arm.Cases)
														{
															<tr class="@(item.Matched ? null : "p0-req")">
																<td><code>@item.Input</code></td><td><small>@item.Expected</small></td>
																<td><small>@item.Actual@(string.IsNullOrEmpty(item.Note) ? "" : $" ({item.Note})")</small></td>
																<td>@(item.Matched ? "match" : "MISMATCH")</td>
															</tr>
														}
													</tbody>
												</table>
											}
											@if (arm.Assumptions.Count > 0)
											{
												<h4>Assumptions the model declared</h4>
												<ul class="rt-list warn">@foreach (var assumption in arm.Assumptions) { <li>@assumption</li> }</ul>
											}
											@if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(arm.Code))
											{
												<pre class="rt-code">@arm.Code</pre>
											}
										</details>
									}
								}
								@if (cc?.Error is { } ccError)
								{
									<div class="compare-status failed" role="alert">@ccError</div>
								}
							</div>
						</section>

						<section class="rt-qa" data-auto="cc.review">
							<header class="rt-head"><span class="rt-step">&#10003;</span><div><strong>Claude Opus 5 reviews the builds against the sealed criteria</strong><small>@(cc?.ReviewModel ?? "review, not grading — the test results are already decided")</small></div></header>
							@if (cc?.Review is { } ccReview)
							{
								<p class="rt-reco">@ccReview.Summary</p>
								<table class="audit-table">
									<thead><tr><th>Arm</th><th>Verdict</th><th>Met</th><th>Missed or at risk</th></tr></thead>
									<tbody>
										@foreach (var reviewed in ccReview.Arms)
										{
											<tr>
												<td><strong>@reviewed.Arm</strong></td><td>@reviewed.Verdict</td>
												<td><ul class="rt-list good">@foreach (var item in reviewed.Strengths) { <li>@item</li> }</ul></td>
												<td><ul class="rt-list warn">@foreach (var item in reviewed.Gaps) { <li>@item</li> }</ul></td>
											</tr>
										}
									</tbody>
								</table>
								<h4>Recommendation</h4>
								<p class="rt-reco">@ccReview.Recommendation</p>
							}
							else if (cc?.Status == "reviewing")
							{
								<div class="rt-pending cc-progress" role="status" aria-live="polite"><span></span><div><strong>Claude is reviewing the builds against the sealed criteria…</strong><small>@ClassifyElapsed since the run started</small></div></div>
							}
							else
							{
								<div class="rt-placeholder">The review appears here once all arms have been compiled and run.</div>
							}

							<h4>The sealed criteria</h4>
							<ol class="rt-list">@foreach (var criterion in ClassifyContextSamples.SealedCriteria) { <li>@criterion</li> }</ol>

							<h4>What this does and does not prove</h4>
							<ul class="rt-list warn">
								<li><strong>Dropping whole sections is not the same as compressing text.</strong> Every kept sentence is verbatim and can be quoted with its section number. That is the property a token-deletion compressor gives up, and it is why this approach fits a programme that insists on citable context.</li>
								<li><strong>Two domains this different are the easy case.</strong> Fixed income and crypto have distinct vocabularies. The honest next test is two adjacent domains &mdash; options versus bonds within the same desk &mdash; where the uncertain band will grow. The size of that band is a result, not an embarrassment.</li>
								<li><strong>One run per arm.</strong> Gemma is not deterministic. A programme decision needs the median of several runs, as the context-sufficiency scene does.</li>
								<li><strong>The classifier is a model too.</strong> Its version is part of the agent release. A new embedding model or a retrained classifier changes what the implementer sees and must be drift-gated like any other model change.</li>
								<li><strong>The same classifier, run per chat turn against the current question, is the production version of this idea.</strong> That is a VSIX-side change once the numbers here hold up.</li>
							</ul>
						</section>
					</section>
					break;
				case 35:
					<section>
						<p class="kicker">REMOVE THE NOISE, OR NAME IT?</p>
						<h2 id="scene-title">Tell the LLM to Focus / Ignore</h2>
						<p class="rt-intro">
							@SceneLink("classify-context") removed the off-topic sections before the model saw them. This scene tests
							the other option: <strong>keep everything, but prepend an instruction that names exactly which sections to
							ignore.</strong> The instruction is written by code from the classifier's decisions &mdash; no person and no
							model composes it &mdash; so it is as deterministic as the filter. The question is whether <em>telling</em> a
							model what to ignore is as good as <em>removing</em> it, and what each costs.
						</p>
						<p class="rt-intro">
							Claude Opus 5 builds the bond settlement service three times: from the mixed document alone, from the mixed
							document with the Focus/Ignore instruction on top, and from the classifier-filtered document as the reference.
							Every build is compiled and executed against the same reference answer. Prompt tokens <em>and output tokens</em>
							are counted, because an instruction that stops the model hedging about the other service saves on the way out,
							not the way in.
						</p>

						@{
							var fx = _session?.Focus;
							var fxMixed = Classifier.MixedDocument();
							var fxMixedTokens = Classifier.Tokens(ContextClassifierService.ToText(fxMixed));
						}

						<section class="rt-box" data-auto="fx.setup">
