														{
															<tr class="@(item.Matched ? null : "p0-req")">
																<td><code>@item.Input</code></td><td><small>@item.Expected</small></td>
																<td><small>@item.Actual@(string.IsNullOrEmpty(item.Note) ? "" : $" ({item.Note})")</small></td>
																<td>@(item.Matched ? "match" : "MISMATCH")</td>
															</tr>
														}
													</tbody>
												</table>
											}
											@if (arm.Assumptions.Count > 0)
											{
												<h4>Assumptions the model declared</h4>
												<ul class="rt-list warn">@foreach (var assumption in arm.Assumptions) { <li>@assumption</li> }</ul>
											}
											@if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(arm.Code))
											{
												<pre class="rt-code">@arm.Code</pre>
											}
										</details>
									}
								}
								@if (fx?.Error is { } fxError)
								{
									<div class="compare-status failed" role="alert">@fxError</div>
								}
							</div>
						</section>

						<section class="rt-qa" data-auto="fx.review">
							<header class="rt-head"><span class="rt-step">&#10003;</span><div><strong>Claude Opus 5 reviews the three builds against the sealed criteria</strong><small>@(fx?.ReviewModel ?? "review, not grading — the test results are already decided")</small></div></header>
							@if (fx?.Review is { } fxReview)
							{
								<p class="rt-reco">@fxReview.Summary</p>
								<table class="audit-table">
									<thead><tr><th>Arm</th><th>Verdict</th><th>Met</th><th>Missed or at risk</th></tr></thead>
									<tbody>
										@foreach (var reviewed in fxReview.Arms)
										{
											<tr>
												<td><strong>@reviewed.Arm</strong></td><td>@reviewed.Verdict</td>
												<td><ul class="rt-list good">@foreach (var item in reviewed.Strengths) { <li>@item</li> }</ul></td>
												<td><ul class="rt-list warn">@foreach (var item in reviewed.Gaps) { <li>@item</li> }</ul></td>
											</tr>
										}
									</tbody>
								</table>
								<h4>Recommendation</h4>
								<p class="rt-reco">@fxReview.Recommendation</p>
							}
							else if (fx?.Status == "reviewing")
							{
								<div class="rt-pending cc-progress" role="status" aria-live="polite"><span></span><div><strong>Claude is reviewing the three builds against the sealed criteria…</strong><small>@FocusElapsed since the run started</small></div></div>
							}
							else
							{
								<div class="rt-placeholder">The review appears here once all three builds have been compiled and run.</div>
							}

							<h4>What this does and does not prove</h4>
							<ul class="rt-list warn">
								<li><strong>Naming the noise never saves input tokens.</strong> Arm I is the largest prompt by construction. It can only pay for itself in output tokens, in correctness, or in the cases where removal is not available &mdash; a hosted chat whose history you cannot edit.</li>
								<li><strong>The instruction is only as good as the classifier.</strong> A section the classifier wrongly labels crypto is now something the model has been <em>told</em> to ignore, which is stronger than merely not seeing it. The same asymmetric rule and reference-following apply for that reason.</li>
								<li><strong>One run per arm, one model.</strong> Instruction-following differs between models; a result for Claude Opus 5 says nothing about Gemma 4 until it is run.</li>
							</ul>
						</section>

						<section class="rt-box" data-auto="fx.future">
							<header class="rt-head"><span class="rt-step">&rarr;</span><div><strong>Future enhancement &mdash; documented, not built</strong><small>the same idea applied to a live VS Code chat</small></div></header>
							<div class="audit-body">
								<p>
									<strong>The goal.</strong> In a long VS Code chat the user drifts between topics, and every later prompt pays for
									every earlier turn. The proposal: when the user sends a prompt, classify every prior turn against <em>that
									prompt</em> and either drop the unrelated turns (the Classify Context approach) or prepend a Focus/Ignore
									instruction naming them (this scene's approach). The user would invoke it explicitly &mdash; a command, then the
									prompt &mdash; so the filtering is a recorded choice, never a silent one.
								</p>
								<h4>Approach</h4>
								<ol class="rt-list">
									<li><strong>Get the history.</strong> The reliable route is a <strong>chat participant</strong> (for example <code>@@ces</code>): VS Code passes the participant its own conversation history on every turn, and the participant calls the model through the <code>vscode.lm</code> API with whatever subset it chooses. An extension cannot edit the history that Copilot Chat sends for its <em>default</em> participant. The attached notes list workarounds for reading the built-in chat &mdash; a proposed <code>vscode.chat</code> session API, a focus / select-all / copy keystroke macro, and attaching Playwright to Electron's remote-debugging port. Treat the proposed API as <em>unverified until checked against the current VS Code release</em>, and treat the macro and debugger routes as demo-only: they read the rendered panel, not the structured turns, and break with UI changes.</li>
									<li><strong>Classify against the current prompt, not fixed classes.</strong> Embed the prompt and each prior turn with embeddinggemma; keep turns above a <em>calibrated</em> similarity threshold, always keep the last N turns, keep any turn that names a file or symbol the prompt names, keep the uncertain band. Same rules as here: asymmetric, reference-following, verbatim.</li>
									<li><strong>Where the classifier runs.</strong> ML.NET does not run in the extension host (Node). Either call the existing C# server over the loopback bridge already used by this training tool, or export the trained model's weights and evaluate them in TypeScript with <code>onnxruntime-node</code> for the embeddings.</li>
									<li><strong>Emit the instruction deterministically</strong>, exactly as Step 3 does: turn numbers and first lines, no summaries, no reasons the classifier does not have.</li>
									<li><strong>Test before trusting.</strong> Replay recorded sessions with sealed expected answers; measure tokens saved <em>and</em> answer quality by execution where possible; include adjacent-topic sessions where the classifier will struggle. Two effects can eat the saving: focused sessions have little to drop, and changing the prefix each turn defeats provider prompt caching.</li>
								</ol>
								<p class="rt-note">
									Governance: the participant makes model calls on the user's behalf, so it is an agent under this programme's
									definition and owes a registry entry, a pinned classifier version, and a drift gate when the embedding model or
									threshold changes.
								</p>
							</div>
						</section>
					</section>
					break;
				case 36:
					<section>
						<p class="kicker">THE RIGHT LLM FOR THE RIGHT JOB</p>
						<h2 id="scene-title">Four models, one hard question, one compiler</h2>
						<p class="rt-intro">
							Leadership is asked to standardise on a model. The honest answer is that <strong>different models are good at
							different jobs</strong>, and the only way to know which is which is to give several the same job and score them
							against something that cannot be argued with. This scene does that for one job the programme cares about &mdash;
							designing a COBOL-to-Java conversion &mdash; and lets a real COBOL compiler decide the part it can decide.
						</p>
						<p class="rt-intro">
							<strong>How it works.</strong> The same brief goes to four models in parallel through the VS Code bridge. Each returns
							a fixed-shape answer: byte offsets, record lengths, the five screen rows the legacy program would print, the hex of
							three packed and binary fields, a design, and &mdash; only if confident &mdash; Java. GnuCOBOL has already compiled and run
							the legacy logic, so every checkable value is marked right or wrong by code. A sealed SME checklist then looks for the
							insights an experienced migration lead would expect. Only after that does a fifth model, seeing anonymised slots, judge
							the design quality. Cost, speed and honesty about uncertainty sit beside the score, because the right model for a job
							is rarely the one that is best at everything.
						</p>

						@{
							var ls = _session?.Shootout;
							var lsOracle = ls?.Oracle ?? Shootout.Oracle;
						}

						<section class="rt-box" data-auto="ls.brief">
							<header class="rt-head"><span class="rt-step">1</span><div><strong>The brief every model receives, word for word</strong><small>IBM Global Auto Mart sample &middot; DCLGEN copybook + one paragraph &middot; no hints about layout or truncation</small></div></header>
							<div class="audit-body">
								<p>
									<strong>Why this question.</strong> It looks easy and is not. A DB2 copybook mixes two-byte binary lengths,
									four-byte binary integers, a packed-decimal price and fixed text. The formatting paragraph moves a signed six-digit
									price into an unsigned five-digit field and a five-digit year into a four-digit one, then moves numbers into text.
									A model that reasons about IBM COBOL semantics gets the bytes right; a model that pattern-matches gets them
									plausibly wrong. Every team migrating COBOL meets exactly this.
								</p>
								<details class="cc-arm">
									<summary><strong>Show the brief</strong> &mdash; DDL, copybook, paragraph 1400, five rows, compiler options</summary>
									<pre class="rt-story">@LlmShootoutSamples.Brief</pre>
								</details>
								<details class="cc-arm">
									<summary><strong>Show the answer contract</strong> &mdash; the JSON shape that makes the answers comparable</summary>
									<pre class="rt-story">@LlmShootoutSamples.AnswerContract</pre>
								</details>
							</div>
						</section>

						<section class="rt-box" data-auto="ls.oracle">
							<header class="rt-head"><span class="rt-step">2</span><div><strong>Ground truth from GnuCOBOL, not from a model</strong><small>@lsOracle.Status</small></div></header>
							<div class="audit-body">
								<p>
									<strong>What we did.</strong> The copybook and the MOVE chain were lifted verbatim into a small harness with no SQL
									and no CICS, compiled with <code>cobc -x -free -std=ibm</code> and run once at server start. Its output is the
									answer key. Offsets are arithmetic over the declared layout and cross-checked against the compiler's
									<code>LENGTH OF</code>. Nothing in this step involves a language model.
								</p>
								@if (lsOracle.Available)
								{
									<div class="audit-scoreline" data-auto="ls.oracle-result">
										<span class="ctx-label ctx-rich">@(2 + lsOracle.Offsets.Count + lsOracle.CaseLines.Count + lsOracle.Hex.Count)</span>
										<div>
											<strong>compiler-checkable facts</strong>
											<small>2 lengths &middot; @lsOracle.Offsets.Count field offsets &middot; @lsOracle.CaseLines.Count output rows &middot; @lsOracle.Hex.Count hex dumps &middot; record @lsOracle.RecordLength bytes, OUTPUS @lsOracle.OutpusLength bytes</small>
										</div>
									</div>
									<table class="audit-table">
										<thead><tr><th>Row</th><th>What the legacy program prints (VINO | YEARO | MODELO | PRICEO | NEWAUTOO)</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead>
										<tbody>
											@foreach (var (line, index) in lsOracle.CaseLines.Select((line, index) => (line, index)))
											{
												<tr><td>CASE@(index + 1)</td><td><code>@line</code></td><td><small>@LsCaseWhy(index)</small></td></tr>
											}
										</tbody>
									</table>
									<table class="audit-table">
										<thead><tr><th>Bytes</th><th>Hex from the compiler</th><th>Reading</th></tr></thead>
										<tbody>
											<tr><td>PRICE = 123456</td><td><code>@lsOracle.Hex.GetValueOrDefault("PRICE_123456")</code></td><td><small>COMP-3, 4 bytes, sign nibble C = positive</small></td></tr>
											<tr><td>PRICE = -1500</td><td><code>@lsOracle.Hex.GetValueOrDefault("PRICE_NEG1500")</code></td><td><small>sign nibble D = negative &mdash; the sign the MOVE then throws away</small></td></tr>
											<tr><td>AUTOYEAR = 2024</td><td><code>@lsOracle.Hex.GetValueOrDefault("AUTOYEAR_2024")</code></td><td><small>COMP, 4 bytes, big-endian</small></td></tr>
											<tr><td>VIN-LEN = 4</td><td><code>@lsOracle.Hex.GetValueOrDefault("VINLEN_4")</code></td><td><small>2-byte binary length prefix of a VARCHAR host variable</small></td></tr>
										</tbody>
									</table>
									<details class="cc-arm">
										<summary><strong>Show the harness and its raw output</strong></summary>
										<pre class="rt-code">@lsOracle.RawOutput</pre>
										<pre class="rt-code">@Shootout.HarnessSource</pre>
									</details>
								}
								else
								{
									<div class="compare-status failed" role="alert">The oracle could not be built: @lsOracle.Status Without it there is nothing to score against, so Step 3 is disabled.</div>
								}
							</div>
						</section>

						<section class="rt-box" data-auto="ls.run-box">
							<header class="rt-head"><span class="rt-step">3</span><div><strong>Ask four models the same question</strong><small>@string.Join(" · ", LlmShootoutSamples.Contestants) &middot; same brief, same system prompt, blind to each other</small></div></header>
							<div class="audit-body">
								<p>
									<strong>What we did.</strong> Four bridge tasks are queued at once, each pinned to a named model. If a model is
									not installed the bridge substitutes one and the table says so &mdash; a substituted result is still a result, but
									it is not a result <em>for that model</em>. Prompt tokens are identical by construction; output tokens and wall-clock
									time are measured per model.
								</p>
								<div class="rt-actions">
									<small>Four model calls over the bridge, then one blinded judge call. Models are not deterministic; this is one run each.</small>
									<button class="primary-command compact" type="button" data-auto="ls.run" disabled="@(!_bridgeConnected || !lsOracle.Available || ShootoutBusy)" @onclick="RunShootout">@ShootoutButtonText</button>
								</div>
								@if (!_bridgeConnected)
								{
									<p class="bridge-required" role="status">Connect the VSIX with the token shown under Local Bridge to run Step 3.</p>
								}
								@if (ShootoutBusy)
								{
