							}
							else
							{
								<div class="rt-placeholder">The verdict appears here after all four answers are scored. The slot-to-model mapping is revealed only at this point.</div>
							}

							<h4>What to take to the leadership meeting</h4>
							<ul class="rt-list">
								<li><strong>Pick models per job, not per vendor.</strong> The scorecard separates <em>knows the domain</em> (compiler checks), <em>thinks like an SME</em> (insights), <em>knows what it does not know</em> (confidence and declined Java) and <em>costs</em> (tokens, seconds). A model can lead one column and trail another; the job decides which column matters.</li>
							<li><strong>Make the checkable part decide first.</strong> Anything a compiler, a test or a diff can settle should never be settled by a model's opinion &mdash; including a judge model's. Here the judge only ranks what is left after the compiler has spoken.</li>
								<li><strong>Repeat this whenever the job or the model changes.</strong> The whole run is one button; the brief, the oracle and the checks are sealed in code. That is what makes it a governance tool rather than a demo.</li>
							</ul>
							<h4>What this does and does not prove</h4>
							<ul class="rt-list warn">
								<li><strong>One run each.</strong> Models are not deterministic. A production selection should run this several times and look at the spread, not one row.</li>
								<li><strong>One job.</strong> This brief tests IBM COBOL storage semantics and migration judgement. A model that wins here may lose on UI code or on summarising a meeting; run the equivalent scene for each job that matters.</li>
								<li><strong>The judge is a model.</strong> Its design scores are an opinion, blinded and constrained, and they sit below the compiler's row for that reason. The keyword insights are a floor: they never award what is absent, but they can miss what is phrased unusually.</li>
								<li><strong>Substitutions are disclosed.</strong> If the bridge could not find a named model it says which model actually answered; a substituted row is not evidence about the named model.</li>
							</ul>
						</section>
					</section>
