				case 37:
					<section>
						<p class="kicker">FASTER TOKENS FROM THE SAME MODEL</p>
						<h2 id="scene-title">Speculative decoding on a 16 GB laptop</h2>
						<p class="rt-intro">
							A large model produces one token per forward pass, and each pass is a full trip through gigabytes of weights.
							<strong>Speculative decoding</strong> lets a small, fast <em>draft</em> guess several tokens ahead, then has the
							large <em>target</em> model check all of the guesses in a single pass. Guesses the target agrees with are kept;
							at the first disagreement the target's own token is used and drafting resumes. The target still decides every
							token, so the technique is advertised as free speed. This scene measures what that claim is worth on the
							machine in front of you &mdash; no cloud, no bridge, no model doing the grading.
						</p>
						<p class="rt-intro">
							<strong>Why it cannot be done across vendors.</strong> Verification compares the draft's tokens against the target's
							probabilities inside one serving process, over one shared vocabulary. A hosted API returns text, not probabilities,
							and the Gemma&nbsp;3 and Gemma&nbsp;4 families do not even share a tokenizer. So a local Gemma cannot draft for
							Claude, or for Gemma&nbsp;4 online; speculative decoding is something a vendor does <em>inside</em> its service, and
							the only version you can run and measure yourself is the one below.
						</p>

						@{
							var sp = _session?.Speculative;
							var spEnv = sp?.Environment;
						}

						<section class="rt-box" data-auto="sp.explain">
							<header class="rt-head"><span class="rt-step">1</span><div><strong>How one decoding step works</strong><small>draft &rarr; verify in one pass &rarr; keep the agreed prefix</small></div></header>
							<div class="audit-body">
								<table class="audit-table">
									<thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>Without speculation</th><th>With a draft model</th></tr></thead>
									<tbody>
										<tr><td>1</td><td>Target computes the next token. One pass through 4B weights.</td><td>Draft (270m) proposes up to 8 tokens in 8 cheap passes.</td></tr>
										<tr><td>2</td><td>Repeat for every token.</td><td>Target scores all 8 positions in <em>one</em> pass through 4B weights.</td></tr>
										<tr><td>3</td><td>&mdash;</td><td>Keep the longest prefix the target agrees with, plus the target's own next token. Discard the rest.</td></tr>
										<tr><td>Cost of 192 tokens</td><td>192 target passes</td><td>192 &divide; (accepted per round + 1) target passes, plus the draft's work</td></tr>
									</tbody>
								</table>
								<p class="rt-note">
									The whole gain comes from the <strong>acceptance rate</strong>. If the draft is right three times out of four, the
									target does a quarter of the passes. If the draft is right half the time, its own cost eats the saving. That is
									why the result is task dependent, and why this scene runs two different prompts.
								</p>
							</div>
						</section>

						<section class="rt-box" data-auto="sp.setup">
							<header class="rt-head"><span class="rt-step">2</span><div><strong>What this laptop has, and what it will run</strong><small>@(spEnv?.Status ?? "probing…")</small></div></header>
							<div class="audit-body">
								@if (spEnv is not null)
								{
									<table class="audit-table">
										<thead><tr><th>Role</th><th>Model file</th><th>On disk</th><th>Note</th></tr></thead>
										<tbody>
											<tr><td>Target</td><td><code>@spEnv.TargetModel</code></td><td>@spEnv.TargetMb.ToString("N0") MB</td><td>Gemma 3 4B instruct, 4-bit weights. The model whose answers we want.</td></tr>
											<tr><td>Draft</td><td><code>@spEnv.DraftModel</code></td><td>@spEnv.DraftMb.ToString("N0") MB</td><td>Gemma 3 270m instruct, 8-bit. Same tokenizer as the target &mdash; the only thing that makes drafting possible.</td></tr>
											<tr><td>Runtime</td><td><code>llama-server.exe</code></td><td>@spEnv.BackendLabel</td><td>The Radeon 840M integrated GPU (460 MB dedicated memory) was measured slower than the CPU for a 4B model, so the CPU build is used.</td></tr>
											<tr><td>Machine</td><td colspan="2">@spEnv.LogicalCores logical cores &middot; @((spEnv.TotalRamMb / 1024.0).ToString("0.0")) GB RAM, @((spEnv.FreeRamMb / 1024.0).ToString("0.0")) GB free now</td><td>Both models plus a 4k context need roughly 3 GB. Close what you can before pressing the button.</td></tr>
										</tbody>
									</table>
									@if (!spEnv.Available)
									{
										<div class="compare-status failed" role="alert">@spEnv.Status Download the two GGUF files into <code>gemma3/</code> and the llama.cpp CPU build into <code>tools/llama-cpp/cpu/</code>, then restart the server.</div>
									}
								}
								<p class="rt-note">
									<strong>Why Gemma 3 and not Gemma 4.</strong> Gemma 4's tokenizer is different from Gemma 3's, so a Gemma 3 270m draft
									cannot serve a Gemma 4 target. The Gemma 4 pair that would work &mdash; E2B drafting for 12B &mdash; needs about
									9&nbsp;GB of weights and does not fit beside everything else on 16&nbsp;GB. The technique is identical; only the
									pair changes.
								</p>
							</div>
						</section>

						<section class="rt-box" data-auto="sp.run-box">
							<header class="rt-head"><span class="rt-step">3</span><div><strong>Run the same prompt three ways</strong><small>greedy decoding &middot; fresh process per arm &middot; llama.cpp's own timings</small></div></header>
							<div class="audit-body">
								<p>
									<strong>What we do.</strong> For each prompt, the target runs alone, then with the 270m draft, then with an
									<em>n-gram</em> draft that needs no second model at all &mdash; it guesses the next tokens from patterns already in
									the text. Sampling is greedy (temperature 0) so any difference in the output is a property of the decoding path,
									not of randomness. One untimed warm-up call on an <em>unrelated</em> prompt settles the freshly loaded weights; the
									second call is measured. The warm-up must be unrelated because the n-gram draft learns from everything the server
									has generated &mdash; warming up on the real prompt would hand it the answer and report a perfect score. Three prompts
									cover the range: fresh code, an edit of existing code, and free prose.
								</p>
								<table class="audit-table">
									<thead><tr><th>Arm</th><th>What runs</th></tr></thead>
									<tbody>
										@foreach (var (arm, label, how) in LocalInferenceService.Arms)
										{
											<tr><td><strong>@label</strong><br /><small><code>@arm</code></small></td><td>@how</td></tr>
										}
									</tbody>
								</table>
								<div class="rt-actions">
									<small>Each button takes two to three minutes on this machine. Only one run at a time.</small>
									@foreach (var prompt in LocalInferenceService.Prompts)
									{
										var existing = sp?.Runs.FirstOrDefault(r => r.PromptKind == prompt.Kind);
										<button class="primary-command compact" type="button" data-auto="sp.run-@prompt.Kind" disabled="@(_session is null || spEnv?.Available != true || SpeculativeBusy)" @onclick="() => RunSpeculative(prompt.Kind)">@(existing is null ? $"Run: {prompt.Title}" : existing.Status == "running" ? "Running…" : $"Run again: {prompt.Title}")</button>
									}
								</div>
								@if (SpeculativeBusy)
								{
									<div class="rt-pending cc-progress" role="status" aria-live="polite" data-auto="sp.progress">
										<span></span>
										<div>
											<strong>@SpeculativeProgressHeadline</strong>
											<small>@SpeculativeProgressDetail</small>
										</div>
									</div>
								}
							</div>
						</section>

						<section class="rt-box" data-auto="sp.results-box">
							<header class="rt-head"><span class="rt-step">4</span><div><strong>Results &mdash; every number is a counter or a stopwatch</strong><small>decode tokens/second &middot; acceptance rate &middot; memory &middot; whether the text matched</small></div></header>
							<div class="audit-body">
								@if (sp is { Runs.Count: > 0 })
								{
									@foreach (var run in sp.Runs)
									{
										var spBase = run.Arms.FirstOrDefault(a => a.Arm == "baseline" && a.Status == "completed");
										<h4 data-auto="sp.results-@run.PromptKind">@run.PromptTitle <small>&mdash; @(run.Status == "running" ? "running" : run.Status) &middot; @((run.FreeRamMbBefore / 1024.0).ToString("0.0")) GB free before the run</small></h4>
										<p class="rt-note"><em>@LocalInferenceService.Prompts.First(p => p.Kind == run.PromptKind).Why</em></p>
										<table class="audit-table">
											<thead><tr><th>Arm</th><th>Tokens</th><th>Decode tok/s</th><th>vs alone</th><th>Drafted</th><th>Accepted</th><th>Accept rate</th><th>Process RAM</th><th>Same text as alone?</th><th>Status</th></tr></thead>
											<tbody>
												@foreach (var (arm, label, _) in LocalInferenceService.Arms)
												{
													var row = run.Arms.FirstOrDefault(a => a.Arm == arm);
