														<td>@(row?.Status == "completed" && spBase is not null && arm != "baseline" ? SpeedDelta(spBase.DecodeTokPerSec, row.DecodeTokPerSec) : "—")</td>
														<td>@(row?.Status == "completed" && arm != "baseline" ? row.Drafted.ToString() : "—")</td>
														<td>@(row?.Status == "completed" && arm != "baseline" ? row.Accepted.ToString() : "—")</td>
														<td>@(row?.AcceptRatePercent is { } rate ? $"{rate}%" : "—")</td>
														<td>@(row?.Status == "completed" ? $"{row.WorkingSetMb:N0} MB" : "—")</td>
														<td>@(row?.Status != "completed" ? "—" : arm == "baseline" ? "reference" : row.IdenticalToBaseline == true ? "identical" : $"differs from char {row.DivergesAtChar}")</td>
														<td>@(row is null ? (run.Status == "running" ? "waiting" : "—") : row.Status == "completed" ? "measured" : row.Error)</td>
													</tr>
												}
											</tbody>
										</table>
										@foreach (var row in run.Arms.Where(a => a.Status == "completed" && a.Text is not null))
										{
											<details class="cc-arm">
												<summary><strong>@row.Label</strong> &mdash; output text (sha @row.Sha)</summary>
												<pre class="rt-code">@row.Text</pre>
											</details>
										}
										@if (run.Error is not null)
										{
											<div class="compare-status failed" role="alert">@run.Error</div>
										}
									}
									<p class="rt-note">
										<strong>How to read it.</strong> <em>Decode tok/s</em> is the number a user feels. <em>Accept rate</em> explains it:
										below roughly 60&nbsp;% the draft's own passes cost more than they save, and the row turns red. <em>Same text</em>
										is the honesty column. The target model chooses every token in all three arms, and the baseline is bit-for-bit
										reproducible run to run &mdash; yet the speculative arms usually diverge. Checking eight positions in one batch
										changes the order of floating-point additions inside the matrix multiplies; where two candidate tokens were
										nearly tied, the argmax flips, and from there the text takes a different but equally valid path. &ldquo;Lossless&rdquo;
										means the same <em>distribution</em>, not the same transcript.
									</p>
								}
								else
								{
									<div class="rt-placeholder">Results appear here as each arm finishes. Run a prompt in Step 3.</div>
								}
								@if (sp?.Error is { } spError)
								{
									<div class="compare-status failed" role="alert">@spError</div>
								}
							</div>
						</section>

						<section class="rt-qa" data-auto="sp.takeaways">
							<header class="rt-head"><span class="rt-step">&#10003;</span><div><strong>What to take from it</strong><small>and what it does not show</small></div></header>
							<h4>For the leadership conversation</h4>
							<ul class="rt-list">
								<li><strong>It is real, and it is task dependent.</strong> Expect the largest gain where the output copies or closely follows the input &mdash; edits, refactors, structured rewrites &mdash; a solid gain on formulaic code, and little or none on free prose. The acceptance-rate column tells you which you have before you commit to it.</li>
								<li><strong>The cheapest draft may be no model at all.</strong> N-gram drafting costs zero extra memory and works whenever the answer repeats the prompt. It does nothing for fresh text, so try it first and keep the draft model for generation.</li>
								<li><strong>You cannot buy this across vendors.</strong> Claude, Gemini and hosted Gemma either do it inside their service or do not; there is no lever on your side. Where it matters is on-device and self-hosted inference, which is exactly where this laptop demonstration applies.</li>
								<li><strong>Verify with a tool, not with your eyes.</strong> The text-identity check is the same discipline as the compiler oracle two scenes back: a claim of &ldquo;identical&rdquo; is a hash comparison, and when it fails the page says at which character.</li>
							</ul>
							<h4>What this does and does not prove</h4>
							<ul class="rt-list warn">
								<li><strong>One machine, one pair.</strong> A discrete GPU changes the arithmetic: the target's batched verification gets much cheaper relative to the draft, and the draft model tends to win over n-gram. Re-run on the target hardware before deciding.</li>
								<li><strong>One run each.</strong> Wall-clock speed on a laptop moves with thermal state and background load; the baseline should be re-run alongside any comparison, as it is here.</li>
								<li><strong>Same distribution is a claim from the algorithm, not from this page.</strong> The page shows that the text can differ; it does not test whether the two outputs are equally good. That would need the task-level oracle approach the rest of the programme uses.</li>
							</ul>
						</section>
					</section>
					break;
				case 38:
					<section>
						<p class="kicker">NOTES → DOMAINS → EDGES</p>
						<h2 id="scene-title">Can the notes rebuild the map?</h2>
						<p class="rt-intro">
							A real modernisation estate is thousands of COBOL programs and tens of millions of lines, and roughly a third
							of those lines are comments. Often the notes are the <em>only</em> description of what a program does. This scene
							tests, on the open <strong>BankDemo</strong> application, whether EmbeddingGemma + ML.NET can read those notes and
							reproduce a documentation set built by hand &mdash; the classification a knowledge graph would need. It uses your
							cortex / neuron / hippocampus framing directly: the catalog is the <em>cortex</em> (the central index), each business
							domain is a <em>neuron</em>, and the call graph between programs is the <em>hippocampus</em> that binds them.
						</p>
						<p class="rt-intro">
							<strong>The ground truth is not ours.</strong> BankDemo ships a hand-written <code>docs/domains</code> set that maps
							every program to a domain and even anticipates this experiment. We score against its <em>Business purpose</em> prose
							only &mdash; never its &ldquo;Classification cues&rdquo; section, which quotes the very notes we are testing, because that
							would be circular. Every number below is a cosine or a held-out prediction; no model grades anything.
						</p>

						@{
							var nc = _session?.NotesClassification;
							var ncr = nc?.Result;
						}

						<section class="rt-box" data-auto="nc.setup">
							<header class="rt-head"><span class="rt-step">1</span><div><strong>The experiment</strong><small>@(nc?.EnvironmentStatus ?? "probing…")</small></div></header>
							<div class="audit-body">
								<p>
									Each program is represented by its <code>* Function:</code> header line plus its distinctive inline notes, with the
									catalog's shared boilerplate removed. We then run five methods of increasing structure and compare each to the
									catalog. The first four use notes alone; the fifth adds the call graph.
								</p>
								<div class="rt-actions">
									<small>Deterministic: embeddinggemma vectors, cosine retrieval, ML.NET SdcaMaximumEntropy and call-graph clustering. About 15&ndash;30 s on this machine; the result is cached.</small>
									<button class="primary-command compact" type="button" data-auto="nc.run" disabled="@(_session is null || nc?.Available != true || NotesBusy)" @onclick="RunNotesClassification">@(ncr is { Available: true } ? "Run again" : "Run the experiment")</button>
								</div>
								@if (nc?.Available != true)
								{
									<div class="compare-status failed" role="alert">@nc?.EnvironmentStatus Set <code>BankDemoRoot</code> to the repository path and ensure embeddinggemma is loaded.</div>
								}
								@if (NotesBusy)
								{
									<div class="rt-pending cc-progress" role="status" aria-live="polite" data-auto="nc.progress">
										<span></span>
										<div><strong>Embedding the notes and the domain descriptions, then running five classifiers…</strong><small>@NotesElapsed elapsed · deterministic, no bridge</small></div>
									</div>
								}
							</div>
						</section>

						@if (ncr is { Available: true })
						{
							<section class="rt-box" data-auto="nc.scores">
								<header class="rt-head"><span class="rt-step">2</span><div><strong>Five methods, one ground truth</strong><small>@ncr.ProgramCount business programs · @ncr.EdgeCount call edges · @ncr.BoilerplatePhrases boilerplate phrases filtered</small></div></header>
								<div class="audit-body">
									<table class="audit-table" data-auto="nc.scores-table">
										<thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>What it uses</th><th>Top-1</th><th>Top-3</th></tr></thead>
										<tbody>
											@foreach (var (score, isStructural) in new[] { (ncr.Retrieval, false), (ncr.RetrievalNoHelp, false), (ncr.Centroid, false), (ncr.Supervised, false), (ncr.Structural, true) })
											{
												<tr class="@(isStructural ? "p0-opt" : null)">
													<td><strong>@score.Name</strong></td>
													<td><small>@score.Method</small></td>
													<td>@score.Top1/@score.Total = <strong>@Pct(score.Top1, score.Total)</strong></td>
													<td>@(score.Top3 == score.Top1 && score.Name.Contains("centroid", StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase) || score.Top3 == score.Top1 ? "—" : $"{score.Top3}/{score.Total} = {Pct(score.Top3, score.Total)}")</td>
												</tr>
											}
										</tbody>
									</table>
									<p class="rt-note">
										<strong>Read it top to bottom.</strong> Plain retrieval scores modestly on top-1 but the right domain is almost
										always in the <em>top 3</em> &mdash; the signal is there. The second row shows the ceiling once the one cross-cutting
										&ldquo;Help&rdquo; domain (next section) is removed as a target. The centroid and supervised rows show what a few
										labelled examples per domain buy. The last row is the honest end-to-end number: same @ncr.ProgramCount programs as
										plain retrieval, but the call graph lifts top-1 by discovering the cross-cutting concern and pooling each feature's
										notes &mdash; no domain is removed by hand.
									</p>
								</div>
							</section>

							<section class="rt-box" data-auto="nc.magnet">
								<header class="rt-head"><span class="rt-step">3</span><div><strong>The cross-cutting magnet (why edges matter)</strong><small>@ncr.CrossCutting.Count program(s) called across many domains</small></div></header>
								<div class="audit-body">
									<p>
										One domain, <em>Online Help</em>, describes itself as &ldquo;implemented inside <strong>every</strong> business
										program&rdquo;. Its description is close to everything, so note-similarity alone routes many programs to it. You cannot
										place a concern that lives everywhere by its words &mdash; you place it by <strong>who calls it</strong>. The call graph
										finds these automatically: a program pulled in by many others, across many domains, is cross-cutting by structure.
									</p>
									<table class="audit-table">
										<thead><tr><th>Program</th><th>Called by (fan-in)</th><th>Caller domains</th><th>Handled by</th></tr></thead>
										<tbody>
											@foreach (var xcut in ncr.CrossCutting)
											{
												<tr><td><code>@xcut.Program</code></td><td>@xcut.FanIn</td><td>@string.Join(", ", xcut.CallerDomains)</td><td><small>@xcut.Role</small></td></tr>
											}
										</tbody>
									</table>
								</div>
							</section>

							<section class="rt-box" data-auto="nc.structure">
								<header class="rt-head"><span class="rt-step">4</span><div><strong>Edges recover the accuracy</strong><small>S/B/D features pooled along LINK edges, classified once</small></div></header>
								<div class="audit-body">
									<p>
										BankDemo's pattern is a triplet: a screen program LINKs a business program, which LINKs a data program. Their
										notes vary in richness &mdash; a data program's &ldquo;Obtain X&rdquo; is thin, the business program's header is strong.
										The call graph groups each triplet into one <em>feature</em>, pools their notes, and classifies the feature once, so
										the thin members ride the strong header. Cross-cutting and shared-data programs are set aside and assigned by their
										callers. Per-domain, notes-only retrieval versus the structure-aware method:
									</p>
									<table class="audit-table">
										<thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Domain</th><th>Programs</th><th>Retrieval correct</th><th>Structure-aware correct</th></tr></thead>
										<tbody>
											@foreach (var d in ncr.DomainScores)
											{
												<tr class="@(d.CorrectStructural > d.CorrectRetrieval ? "p0-opt" : d.CorrectStructural < d.CorrectRetrieval ? "p0-req" : null)">
													<td>@d.Number</td><td>@d.Title</td><td>@d.Programs</td>
													<td>@d.CorrectRetrieval / @d.Programs</td><td><strong>@d.CorrectStructural / @d.Programs</strong></td>
												</tr>
											}
										</tbody>
									</table>
									<p class="rt-note">
										The greens are where pooling along the call graph corrected a note-only miss; several domains go to a perfect
										score once the thin data-program notes ride the strong business header. The one place structure loses is
										&ldquo;More Information&rdquo;, which genuinely <em>is</em> Help: because Help has to be excluded as a target to stop it
										swallowing everything, those two programs land elsewhere &mdash; a fair trade of two edge cases against the roughly
										thirty the magnet would otherwise capture.
									</p>
									<details class="cc-arm">
										<summary><strong>Per-program detail</strong> &mdash; truth, retrieval prediction (rank of the true domain) and the structure-aware prediction</summary>
										<table class="audit-table">
											<thead><tr><th></th><th>Program</th><th>Layer</th><th>Truth</th><th>Retrieval</th><th>true rank</th><th>Structure</th><th>notes</th><th>Function</th></tr></thead>
											<tbody>
												@foreach (var row in ncr.Rows)
												{
													<tr class="@(row.StructuralPred == row.TruthDomain ? null : "p0-req")">
														<td>@(row.CrossCutting ? "×-cut" : "")</td>
														<td><code>@row.Name</code></td><td>@row.Layer</td>
														<td>@row.TruthDomain</td>
														<td>@(row.RetrievalPred == row.TruthDomain ? row.RetrievalPred : $"{row.RetrievalPred} ✗")</td>
														<td>@(row.RetrievalTruthRank < 0 ? "?" : row.RetrievalTruthRank.ToString())</td>
														<td>@(row.StructuralPred == row.TruthDomain ? row.StructuralPred : $"{row.StructuralPred} ✗")</td>
														<td>@row.DistinctiveCount/@row.NoteCount</td>
														<td><small>@Trunc(row.Function, 30)</small></td>
													</tr>
												}
											</tbody>
										</table>
									</details>
								</div>
							</section>

							<section class="rt-qa" data-auto="nc.takeaways">
								<header class="rt-head"><span class="rt-step">&#10003;</span><div><strong>What this means for the migration</strong><small>and the honest limits</small></div></header>
								<h4>For the leadership conversation</h4>
								<ul class="rt-list">
									<li><strong>The notes carry the domain.</strong> The correct domain sits in the top three for the large majority of programs; the embedding space separates the business functions from the comments alone.</li>
									<li><strong>The <code>Function:</code> header line is the workhorse.</strong> Stripping body boilerplate barely moved the score because that one governed header line does most of the work. On a real estate, auditing that those headers exist and are honest is the highest-value first step.</li>
									<li><strong>Neurons need the hippocampus.</strong> Cross-cutting concerns cannot be placed by words; the call graph places them, and pooling notes along the call edges lifts the genuine business domains toward the high band. Notes give the neurons; edges give the binding.</li>
									<li><strong>This is a floor, not a ceiling.</strong> BankDemo gives only 2&ndash;4 programs per domain. At estate scale &mdash; thousands of programs, dozens per domain &mdash; the supervised classifier has far more to learn from, and top-3 already shows the space is separable.</li>
								</ul>
								<h4>What this does and does not prove</h4>
								<ul class="rt-list warn">
									<li><strong>One clean, well-documented app.</strong> Your estate will have missing, copied and misleading headers; accuracy must keep being measured against a held-out sealed sample as you scale, the same discipline as the compiler oracle in the other scenes.</li>
									<li><strong>The ground truth is human and imperfect too.</strong> The catalog notes its own copy-paste errors and undecided layers; some &ldquo;misses&rdquo; are arguable rather than wrong.</li>
									<li><strong>Edges here are LINK/CALL/COPY only.</strong> A production graph would add data-flow and dynamic routing; more edges, better binding.</li>
								</ul>
							</section>
						}
						@if (nc?.Error is { } ncError)
						{
							<div class="compare-status failed" role="alert">@ncError</div>
						}
					</section>
					break;
				case 39:
					<section>
						<p class="kicker">A MODEL IN THE ROOM, NOT IN THE CLOUD</p>
						<h2 id="scene-title">LLM in the Room</h2>
						<p class="rt-intro">
							A team is working through a topic together and wants a model in the room: one that listens when someone
							presses the microphone, answers in a sentence or three, asks a question back, and keeps the notes. This scene
							runs the whole loop on this laptop &mdash; <strong>whisper.cpp</strong> turns speech into text, <strong>Gemma 4
							E2B</strong> answers through llama.cpp on the CPU, the browser reads the answer aloud sentence by sentence
							&mdash; with no cloud model and no VS Code bridge. Nothing said in the room leaves the machine.
						</p>
						<p class="rt-intro">
							The topic is <strong>@RoomChatService.Topic</strong>. Gemma is grounded on the fixture's own OKF explanation and
							is told to answer only from it, so ask it what OKF is, what <code>stale_after</code> means, why the API field is
							missing on purpose, or whether reading a bundle trains the model. Every answer ends with a question back to you.
						</p>

						@{
							var rm = Room.Snapshot();
							var rmEnv = rm.Environment;
							var rmLast = rm.Turns.LastOrDefault(t => t.Role == "assistant" && t.Status == "completed");
							var rmAnswers = rm.Turns.Where(t => t.Role == "assistant" && t.Status == "completed").ToList();
						}

						<section class="rt-box" data-auto="rm.setup">
							<header class="rt-head"><span class="rt-step">1</span><div><strong>What runs, and where</strong><small>@rmEnv.Status</small></div></header>
							<div class="audit-body">
								<table class="audit-table">
									<thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>Runs on</th><th>What</th><th>Why this choice</th></tr></thead>
									<tbody>
										<tr><td>Listen</td><td>Browser</td><td>Microphone captured at 16 kHz mono while the button is lit; sent as WAV to the loopback server</td><td>No audio library on the server side; the browser already has the microphone</td></tr>
										<tr><td>Transcribe</td><td>whisper.cpp, CPU</td><td><code>@rmEnv.WhisperModel</code> (@rmEnv.WhisperMb MB), English</td><td>About a second for a ten-second question on this machine; the base model is accurate enough for spoken questions</td></tr>
										<tr><td>Answer</td><td>llama.cpp, CPU, @rmEnv.Threads threads</td><td><code>@rmEnv.ModelFile</code> (@rmEnv.ModelMb.ToString("N0") MB), thinking off, n-gram draft, prompt cache reuse</td><td>Measured at about 20 tokens a second here; 4-bit because CPU decode is bound by memory bandwidth, so half the bytes is nearly twice the speed</td></tr>
										<tr><td>Speak</td><td>Browser</td><td>Each completed sentence is queued to the browser's speech engine as it arrives</td><td>The room hears the first sentence while the rest is still being generated</td></tr>
										<tr><td>Record</td><td>Server memory</td><td>Verbatim transcript with speaker and timings; running notes rewritten by Gemma between questions</td><td>The transcript is the record. The notes are model-generated and say so</td></tr>
									</tbody>
								</table>
								<p class="rt-note">
									<strong>Why not speculative decoding with a draft model?</strong> @SceneLink("speculative-decoding") measured it on
									this laptop: a small draft helps on code and slows prose down. Conversation is prose. The speed here comes from
									sending less (a fixed grounding prefix the server caches, plus the last few turns) and saying less (three
									sentences, 160-token cap). The n-gram draft is kept because it costs nothing and helps when the answer quotes the notes.
								</p>
								<div class="rt-actions">
									<small>Both processes are launched on this host and stopped with the server. About 3.5 GB of RAM while running; @((rmEnv.FreeRamMb / 1024.0).ToString("0.0")) GB free now.</small>
									@if (rm.Status is "offline" or "error")
									{
										<button class="primary-command compact" type="button" data-auto="rm.start" disabled="@(!rmEnv.Available)" @onclick="StartRoom">Start the room</button>
									}
									else
									{
										<button class="secondary-command compact" type="button" data-auto="rm.stop" @onclick="StopRoom">Stop the room</button>
									}
								</div>
								@if (rm.Status is "starting" or "warming")
								{
									<div class="rt-pending cc-progress" role="status" aria-live="polite"><span></span><div><strong>@(rm.Status == "starting" ? "Loading Gemma 4 and whisper…" : "Pre-filling the grounding so the first question does not pay for it…")</strong><small>@RoomElapsed elapsed</small></div></div>
								}
								@if (rm.Status == "error" && rm.Error is { } rmError)
								{
									<div class="compare-status failed" role="alert">@rmError</div>
								}
								@if (rm.Status is "ready" or "answering" or "noting")
								{
									<div class="compare-status" role="status" data-auto="rm.ready">Ready. The grounding is @rm.GroundingTokens.ToString("N0") prompt tokens and is already cached on the server.</div>
								}
							</div>
						</section>

						<section class="rt-box" data-auto="rm.chat-box">
							<header class="rt-head"><span class="rt-step">2</span><div><strong>Talk to Gemma</strong><small>press the microphone, ask, press again to send &middot; or type</small></div></header>
							<div class="audit-body">
								<div class="room-controls">
									<label class="room-speaker">Who is speaking<input type="text" maxlength="40" @bind="_roomSpeaker" placeholder="Team" /></label>
									<button class="@("room-mic" + (_roomRecording ? " recording" : ""))" type="button" data-auto="rm.mic"
											title="@(_roomRecording ? "Stop and send" : "Press and ask")" aria-label="@(_roomRecording ? "Stop recording and send" : "Start recording")"
											disabled="@(!Room.IsReady || _roomTranscribing)" @onclick="ToggleRoomMic">
										<svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path fill="currentColor" d="M12 15a4 4 0 0 0 4-4V6a4 4 0 1 0-8 0v5a4 4 0 0 0 4 4zm6-4a6 6 0 0 1-12 0H4a8 8 0 0 0 7 7.93V22h2v-3.07A8 8 0 0 0 20 11z"/></svg>
										<span>@(_roomRecording ? $"Listening… {RoomRecordingSeconds}s — press to send" : _roomTranscribing ? "Transcribing…" : "Press and ask")</span>
									</button>
									<label class="room-toggle"><input type="checkbox" @bind="_roomSpeakAloud" /> Read answers aloud</label>
								</div>
								<div class="room-typed">
									<textarea class="rt-code-input" rows="2" maxlength="600" data-auto="rm.typed" placeholder="Or type a question for the room…" @bind="_roomTyped" @bind:event="oninput" disabled="@(!Room.IsReady)" aria-label="Typed question"></textarea>
									<button class="primary-command compact" type="button" data-auto="rm.send" disabled="@(!Room.IsReady || string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(_roomTyped))" @onclick="SendRoomTyped">Send</button>
								</div>
								@if (!_roomMicSupported)
								{
									<p class="bridge-required" role="status">This browser did not grant microphone access. Typing still works; use Edge or Chrome for speech.</p>
								}
								@if (_roomMicError is { } micError)
								{
									<div class="compare-status failed" role="alert">@micError</div>
								}

								<div class="room-transcript" id="room-transcript" data-auto="rm.transcript">
									@if (rm.Turns.Count == 0)
									{
										<div class="rt-placeholder">@(Room.IsReady ? "Ask the first question. Try: \u201cWhat is OKF, in one breath?\u201d" : "Start the room, then ask a question.")</div>
									}
									@foreach (var turn in rm.Turns)
									{
										<div class="@("room-turn " + (turn.Role == "user" ? "user" : "model") + (turn.Status == "streaming" ? " streaming" : turn.Status == "failed" ? " failed" : ""))">
											<div class="room-turn-head">
												<strong>@turn.Speaker</strong>
												<small>
													@turn.At.ToLocalTime().ToString("HH:mm:ss")
													@if (turn.Role == "user" && turn.SttMs > 0) { <text>&middot; transcribed in @turn.SttMs ms</text> }
													@if (turn.Role == "assistant" && turn.Status == "completed") { <text>&middot; first word @turn.FirstTokenMs ms &middot; @turn.OutputTokens tokens in @((turn.TotalMs / 1000.0).ToString("0.0")) s &middot; @turn.TokPerSec tok/s &middot; @turn.CachedTokens of @turn.PromptTokens prompt tokens from cache</text> }
													@if (turn.Status == "streaming") { <text>&middot; answering…</text> }
												</small>
											</div>
											<p>@(turn.Status == "failed" ? (turn.Error ?? "The answer did not complete.") : turn.Text)@if (turn.Status == "streaming") { <span class="room-caret">&#9612;</span> }</p>
										</div>
									}
								</div>
								@if (rm.Error is { } rmChatError && rm.Status != "error")
								{
									<div class="compare-status failed" role="alert">@rmChatError</div>
								}
								<div class="rt-actions">
									<small>@rm.Turns.Count(t => t.Role == "user") question(s) &middot; the prompt carries the grounding plus the last 10 turns, nothing else.</small>
									<button class="secondary-command compact" type="button" data-auto="rm.clear" disabled="@(rm.Turns.Count == 0)" @onclick="ClearRoom">Clear the conversation</button>
								</div>
							</div>
						</section>

						<section class="rt-box" data-auto="rm.latency">
							<header class="rt-head"><span class="rt-step">3</span><div><strong>Was it fast enough?</strong><small>every figure is a stopwatch or a llama.cpp counter</small></div></header>
							<div class="audit-body">
								@if (rmAnswers.Count > 0)
								{
									<table class="audit-table" data-auto="rm.latency-table">
										<thead><tr><th>Measure</th><th>Target for a room</th><th>Last answer</th><th>Median so far</th></tr></thead>
										<tbody>
											<tr class="@(rmLast!.FirstTokenMs <= 1500 ? "p0-opt" : "p0-req")"><td>First word on screen</td><td>&le; 1.5 s</td><td>@rmLast.FirstTokenMs ms</td><td>@Median(rmAnswers.Select(t => (double)t.FirstTokenMs)).ToString("0") ms</td></tr>
											<tr class="@(rmLast.TotalMs <= 10000 ? "p0-opt" : "p0-req")"><td>Whole answer</td><td>&le; 10 s</td><td>@((rmLast.TotalMs / 1000.0).ToString("0.0")) s</td><td>@((Median(rmAnswers.Select(t => (double)t.TotalMs)) / 1000.0).ToString("0.0")) s</td></tr>
											<tr class="@(rmLast.TokPerSec >= 15 ? "p0-opt" : "p0-req")"><td>Decode speed</td><td>&ge; 15 tok/s</td><td>@rmLast.TokPerSec tok/s</td><td>@Median(rmAnswers.Select(t => t.TokPerSec)).ToString("0.0") tok/s</td></tr>
											<tr class="@(rmLast.OutputTokens <= 160 ? "p0-opt" : "p0-req")"><td>Answer length</td><td>&le; 160 tokens</td><td>@rmLast.OutputTokens tokens</td><td>@Median(rmAnswers.Select(t => (double)t.OutputTokens)).ToString("0") tokens</td></tr>
											<tr class="@(rmLast.PromptTokens > 0 && rmLast.CachedTokens * 100 / Math.Max(1, rmLast.PromptTokens) >= 80 ? "p0-opt" : "p0-req")"><td>Prompt served from cache</td><td>&ge; 80 %</td><td>@(rmLast.PromptTokens == 0 ? "—" : $"{rmLast.CachedTokens * 100 / Math.Max(1, rmLast.PromptTokens)} %")</td><td>@rm.GroundingTokens.ToString("N0") tokens of grounding never re-read</td></tr>
										</tbody>
									</table>
									<p class="rt-note">
										The first word is what the room feels; it is transcription time plus the prefill of whatever is <em>not</em>
										cached &mdash; the new question and the last answer. Decode speed decides how long the room waits for the
										question Gemma asks back. Red rows are the honest result on this hardware, not a bug.
									</p>
								}
								else
								{
									<div class="rt-placeholder">Latency figures appear here after the first answer.</div>
								}
							</div>
						</section>

						<section class="rt-box" data-auto="rm.notes">
							<header class="rt-head"><span class="rt-step">4</span><div><strong>Running notes</strong><small>@(rm.Notes.Status == "updating" ? "Gemma is rewriting the notes between questions…" : rm.Notes.UpdatedAt is { } upd ? $"version {rm.Notes.Version} · {upd.ToLocalTime():HH:mm:ss} · generated by Gemma 4, verified by nobody yet" : "written after the second exchange")</small></div></header>
							<div class="audit-body">
								<p class="rt-note">
									The scribe lane runs only while nobody is asking: pressing the microphone cancels it, and it starts again after the
									next answer. The notes are rewritten from the verbatim transcript each time, so they never drift from what was said.
									They carry <code>generated: gemma-4-e2b-it-Q4_K_M</code> and <code>verified: null</code> until a person signs them off.
								</p>
								@if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(rm.Notes.Markdown))
								{
									<pre class="rt-story" data-auto="rm.notes-text">---
type: Meeting Notes
topic: @RoomChatService.Topic
generated: { by: "model:gemma-4-e2b-it-Q4_K_M", at: "@(rm.Notes.UpdatedAt?.ToString("u") ?? "")" }
verified: null
sources: [transcript]
---
@rm.Notes.Markdown</pre>
								}
								else if (rm.Notes.Error is { } notesError)
								{
									<div class="compare-status failed" role="alert">@notesError</div>
								}
								else
								{
									<div class="rt-placeholder">Notes appear here once the conversation has at least one question and one answer.</div>
								}
							</div>
						</section>

						<section class="rt-qa" data-auto="rm.takeaways">
							<header class="rt-head"><span class="rt-step">&#10003;</span><div><strong>What this shows, and what it does not</strong><small>for the room and for the budget</small></div></header>
							<h4>For the leadership conversation</h4>
							<ul class="rt-list">
								<li><strong>A useful model fits in the room.</strong> A 3 GB open-weight model on a laptop CPU answers a grounded question in a few seconds and asks a sensible one back. No per-token invoice, no data leaving the building.</li>
								<li><strong>Latency is a design choice, not a model property.</strong> Cache the grounding, send only the last few turns, cap the answer, speak sentence by sentence. The same model with the whole transcript in every prompt would feel three times slower.</li>
								<li><strong>Grounding is what makes it safe to put in front of a team.</strong> Gemma is told to answer only from the OKF notes and to say when they do not cover something. Ask it about something else and watch it decline.</li>
								<li><strong>The transcript is the record; the notes are a draft.</strong> The same rule as everywhere in this programme: model output carries <code>verified: null</code> until a person changes it.</li>
							</ul>
							<h4>What this does and does not prove</h4>
							<ul class="rt-list warn">
								<li><strong>One microphone, one speaker at a time.</strong> The presser's name is the speaker label; there is no diarization. In a real room that is a shared microphone and a name field, not a solved problem.</li>
								<li><strong>English, base model.</strong> whisper base.en is fast and adequate for clear speech at a metre. Accents, cross-talk and jargon will degrade it; the transcript shows what was heard, so a wrong hearing is visible rather than silent.</li>
								<li><strong>One topic, one grounding pack.</strong> The tutor is only as good as the notes it is given. Swapping the topic is swapping the pack &mdash; and the pack needs an owner and a <code>stale_after</code>.</li>
								<li><strong>This is an agent.</strong> It makes model calls on the team's behalf and records what people say. It owes a registry entry, a data-tier decision on the transcript, and a pinned model hash &mdash; see @SceneLink("phase-zero").</li>
							</ul>
						</section>
					</section>
					break;
			}
		</article>

		<footer class="stage-footer">
			<button class="secondary-command" type="button" disabled="@(_scene == 0)" @onclick="Previous">&larr; Previous</button>
			<div class="narration-text">
